3 Description
*************

oc-box starts unmodified OpenCode and its local MCP servers, plugins,
formatters, shell, and file tools together inside a persistent
development container.  It associates one environment with one canonical
project directory.  The entire project, including hidden files, Git
history, untracked files, and unfinished edits, is mounted writable at
the same absolute path.

The container HOME has the host user's home pathname but uses a private
named volume.  It is not a mount of the host home.  The live OpenCode
database, session IDs, snapshots, caches, and home-installed
dependencies persist in this volume.  Packages installed elsewhere in
the OS persist in the container's writable layer until that container is
reset or removed.

On normal exit, oc-box exports conversation history to separate archive
sessions in the normal host OpenCode database, then stops the container.
Launching oc-box again resumes the original container session.  Running
processes do not survive a stop.  Host OpenCode and its configuration
are unchanged.  On V2, ordinary tool permission asks for the actual
container Build agent are auto-allowed; explicit
session/mandatory-policy denials remain denied, and Plan/subagents
retain their own rules.  Copied ordinary global/Build-agent rules are
overridden by the existing final Build wildcard, as before.  V1's
existing Build permission behavior is unchanged.

The default resource limits are 4 CPU cores, 8 GiB RAM, and 2048
processes.  These are ceilings, not reservations: resources are consumed
dynamically up to those limits, and OpenCode itself has no fixed 4/8 GiB
minimum requirement.  There is no disk quota.  Containers share the host
kernel and can reach network services, including configured host
services at ‘host.containers.internal’.  The project is intentionally
writable and can be deleted by container commands.  Additional Podman
options and devices explicitly extend these defaults.

3.1 OpenCode versions
=====================

oc-box 1.5 supports OpenCode 1.x and 2.x, tested with 1.19.5 and 2.2.6.
The host release selects the exact container package: ‘opencode-ai’ for
1.x, ‘@opencode/cli’ for 2.x.  V2 uses a workspace-owned private stdio
server with native ‘--server’ clients and current credentials at each
new owning startup.  Without an explicit workspace policy, V2 defaults
to all current global vault entries when the host vault is present; V1
retains legacy env-file forwarding.  Isolated setup/history probes use
native OpenCode ‘--standalone’, not an oc-box flag.  The host shared
service is never restarted or exposed inside the container.  Its service
settings are not copied.  V2 uses a separate container database/resume
state seeded from a consistent V1 snapshot and migrated by upstream; V1
history is retained for downgrade.

Native V2 plugins use the new API; copied V1 local plugins get bounded
adapters for shell environment, tool execution/output/definition, text
custom tools and provider discovery.  Unsupported hooks still require
native ports.  Supported legacy config and V2 fields are retained; the
Build plugin is version-aware.  MCP Code Mode and native formatting are
preserved.  Upstream 2.2.6 does not run language servers or expose LSP
diagnostics; native LSP remains on 1.x.  See ‘docs/opencode-2.md’ for
environment strategy, migration limits, and accepted host validation.


