4 Commands
**********

4.1 completion
==============

Print the bundled shell completion script to stdout.  Bash is currently
the only supported shell: ‘oc-box completion bash’.  This informational
command needs no Podman, OpenCode, network connection, host credential
access, or workspace and can run inside the container.  ‘oc-box
completion --help’ describes the syntax.  See Bash completion under
Installation and manuals for activation and install paths.  It never
edits shell startup files automatically.

     source <(oc-box completion bash)

4.2 doctor
==========

Report prerequisites by feature, missing commands, and installation
commands for the OS running doctor.  It is read-only: no packages
installed, network requests, Podman operations, workspace/config
changes, or secret prompts.  Run on the host for host advice.  Inside
oc-box it explicitly reports ‘current-container’; this cannot inspect
host packages.  A workspace's selected distro does not determine host
package guidance.  Unknown hosts list required providers without
guessing a package manager from the default workspace image or executing
os-release text.

     oc-box doctor
     oc-box doctor --feature private-output
     oc-box doctor --feature backup --feature vault
     oc-box doctor --feature backup-info --json

Feature selectors: ‘launch’, ‘private-output’, ‘maintenance’, ‘history’,
‘backup’, ‘backup-info’, ‘restore’, ‘vault’, ‘github-upgrade’,
‘bootstrap’, ‘storage’, ‘overlay’, ‘manuals’, and ‘docs-build’.  Without
selectors all are reported, but exit status checks only core ‘launch’
prerequisites.  With selectors, exit 0 means every selected feature's
prerequisites are present; exit 1 means at least one is missing or
incompatible.  JSON schema version 1 includes scope, detected distro,
feature availability, dependency details, missing providers, package
names, installation commands, and notes.  Missing optional features
never disable unrelated commands.  Feature commands check their
prerequisites before secret prompts or destructive workspace changes and
include OS-specific fixes.

This checks installed prerequisites, not full runtime certification.
Podman still checks rootless mode, subordinate IDs, controller
delegation, and mounts; interactive commands check terminal
requirements.  Command presence does not establish a compatible version
or validate every backend/driver configuration.  Normal OpenCode launch
verifies its host/container versions separately.

‘private-output’ probes actual Linux anonymous-memory file creation
using the same native-Python/libc path as credential capture and
backup/restore.  V2 owning launches require it even with ‘--no-vault’;
ordinary V1 launches without vault values and attach-only clients do
not.  It is separate from the version-neutral ‘launch’ prerequisites:
doctor does not start OpenCode to infer its version.

4.3 start
=========

Create or resume an environment.  This is the default when no command is
given.  The project directory must already exist and be dedicated to the
project.  Without a selector, use the current directory.  With
‘--workspace’, use the registered directory; a workspace ID takes
precedence if both selectors are provided.  Do not combine selectors
when a single selector will do.

On first launch, detect the host distro, create a labeled container and
private home volume, install baseline tools, install the exact host
OpenCode release, copy configuration, resolve plugin dependencies, and
start a conversation.  Afterward, start the existing environment and
resume its latest recorded root conversation.  ‘--new’ starts another
conversation in the same environment.

     mkdir -p ~/devel/demo
     oc-box --project ~/devel/demo
     oc-box start --project ~/devel/demo --new

For V2, initialize and populate the host vault **before the first
project launch** to use the global default immediately; see secrets
below for installation and masked input.  No per-project credential
registration is needed.  Each new owner unlocks once and delivers all
current entries unless an explicit env/selected policy overrides it.
Changes to vault values/names apply on the next new owner, not to
attached clients.  With no vault, env-file fallback remains available
with concise setup guidance.  Native OpenCode saved/OAuth credential
refresh/copy remains independent in every mode.  ‘--no-vault’ opts out
for one owning V2 launch without saving policy.  Configured vault
failures abort before setup; they do not silently fall back to the
environment.

Cold startup includes image/package/plugin downloads; timed stage
messages appear every five seconds.  A loading line covers blank TUI
startup frames and disappears when OpenCode draws text.  Warm launches
retain compatible caches.  The container OpenCode version is verified
against the host on every launch; exact upgrades and downgrades are
supported, with no fallback to another release.

4.4 stop
========

Stop a registered container without deleting the project, container OS,
private home, session IDs, or metadata.  Already stopped or missing
containers need no start.  This command can run from a second host
terminal while OpenCode is active.  Its processes stop, and the active
client may disconnect or report tool errors.  Exit that launcher before
starting another instance or resetting the workspace.  Use ‘sync’
afterward if a final history export was missed.

For V2, this command records stop intent before waiting for a private
transition lock (up to 45 seconds).  A same-version owning launcher then
suppresses automatic post-stop history recovery rather than undoing the
successful stop.  A start whose engine outcome could not be verified
leaves a private uncertainty fence: stop is still attempted, but no
successful-stop message is printed and the command returns nonzero.  Do
not delete control files to bypass that refusal; verify the host state
before repairing the control condition.  Older launchers or direct
Podman commands do not participate in this coordination protocol.  V1
stop behavior is unchanged.

     oc-box stop --project ~/devel/demo

4.5 sync
========

Refresh host conversation archives without opening the TUI. Requires an
existing workspace and an intact project directory.  It starts or
recreates the container if needed, prepares its runtime/configuration,
discovers saved sessions, exports them, and imports separate namespaced
archive copies into the host database.  It stops the container
afterward, even if it had been running.

Manual ‘sync’ retains ordinary runtime preparation and native service
startup, including configured integrations and native scheduling
behavior.  It is not the isolated, non-executing post-stop reader used
by automatic timeout recovery.

On V2, discovery reads every native API page at the workspace's internal
project directory, including archived conversations, forks, and linked
child/subagent sessions.  Separate conversations are never merged.
Sessions at other locations, including nested directories, are outside
this exact-directory scope.  Automatic resume still selects a top-level
conversation, not a recently active child.  A successful complete
inventory removes missing/deleted or moved-away sources from active
export tracking while retaining their last private and host archives.
An unavailable or invalid inventory is an error, never evidence of
deletion.

V2 archives remain independent top-level host conversations.  Parent and
fork provenance is retained in versioned
‘info.metadata["oc-box.archive"]’ annotations, with workspace-namespaced
session and fork-boundary message IDs.  These annotations are
informational, not authority to restore permissions, select projects, or
delete conversations.  Other metadata is preserved; an existing value at
this reserved key is retained under the annotation's ‘sourceMetadata’
field.  Native parent links, fork state, permissions, and revert
controls are not reactivated.  Replacing an archive refuses observed
native host child links, because native deletion would recursively
remove those conversations.  Successful V2 imports are re-exported to
verify transcript and metadata before marking them synchronized.  Treat
generated host conversations as archive mirrors, not bidirectional
working copies.  Do not edit them or create native children while sync
runs: the child preflight and replacement are not an atomic transaction
with unrelated host clients.

An active oc-box launcher holds a workspace lock, so exit it before
syncing.  Use this after an OpenCode crash, missed exit-time sync, or
host restart.  Sync failure preserves container data; retry after
resolving the error.  V2 exports are checked against the paginated
projected transcript.  An unfinished tail assistant, running user
shell/compaction, or changing transcript leaves that session pending,
while other sessions can still synchronize.  In an inactive session, an
unfinished assistant followed by settled messages is superseded: sync
mirrors native's export and annotates the omitted count.  The exception
is refused while that session is active.  See the Sessions and recovery
section.  History archives are not full backups of pending input,
execution state, or persistent shell terminals; use encrypted workspace
backup for full private state.

     oc-box sync --project ~/devel/demo
     oc-box sync --workspace WORKSPACE_ID

4.6 web — browser interface
===========================

Open the native OpenCode 2.x browser UI for a workspace.  If its TUI or
web owner is running, attach to that same private service/session
without waiting for it to exit or unlocking/refreshing credentials.
Otherwise perform a normal V2 startup (including global or selected
vault unlock) and own a foreground web lease.  New projects can be
created using the host's default distro.  Requires host OpenCode 2.x;
1.x has no web command through this launcher.

     oc-box --project ~/devel/demo
     # While it runs, use another host terminal:
     oc-box web --project ~/devel/demo
     oc-box terminal --project ~/devel/demo

The browser uses upstream's short-lived one-use pairing link and opens
the same session through the native web app.  A host relay binds only
‘127.0.0.1’, forwards HTTP/streaming/WebSocket bytes through Podman exec
to container loopback, and does not publish a container port, allow LAN
access, or join the host OpenCode service.  Links grant access to the
workspace service; do not share or log them.  ‘--no-open’ prints the
link without launching a browser.  ‘--port’ defaults to 0 (choose a free
loopback port).  Keep the web command open; Ctrl-C closes that relay.
Browser commands use a sanitized host environment without provider/vault
values; the server retains the owning launch's credential snapshot.

The owner keeps the lifecycle lock and waits for attached
terminal/web/TUI clients before final history sync and container stop.
Closing one attached client does not stop the others.  ‘oc-box stop’
from another host terminal stops the whole workspace and disconnects all
clients.  Backup/reset/purge continue to require the lifecycle lock.
Run normal ‘oc-box start --project PATH’ to join a running web-owned
session with the full TUI. Additional clients accept selectors (and
start's ‘--resume’) only; change model/session/configuration when no
owner is active.  The owning foreground command is needed for web
access; closing the browser tab alone does not close its relay or
terminate the owner.  After the last client exits, V2 final sync waits
up to 30 seconds for the same private server's foreground sessions and
unconsumed background jobs to settle.  An idle persistent shell terminal
does not block this wait.  Timeout or an invalid activity response
leaves history sync pending and returns nonzero; normal cleanup still
stops the container, so running work is not promised to continue.  This
is bounded checking, not an atomic freeze of every possible history
producer.  On timeout, a separate diagnostic reports the wait phase
(‘foreground-query’, ‘background-query’ or ‘poll-delay’) and counts from
the last complete, validated activity/job observation.  Job counts
distinguish running, completed, error and cancelled entries; counts
above 9999 appear as ‘9999+’.  If no complete pair was validated, the
observation is ‘unavailable’, not zero.  These are previous
observations, not proof of the current state or that a native request
succeeded: a request failure at the deadline also retains the
‘activity-timeout’ category.  No session/job identifiers, commands,
transcript text or native errors are printed.

Native OpenCode 2.2.6/2.2.7 can retain a cancelled background-shell job
after a location reload, even with no foreground activity.  Such a row
still blocks the wait; it is not safe to ignore terminal-status jobs
generally because their completion notices can restart foreground work.
A successful later ‘oc-box sync’ checks native exports without the
original owner's activity wait and does not prove that its retained job
disappeared.  The native job-cleanup issue is not repaired by the
diagnostic or by the persisted-history snapshot described below.

For explicitly supported native 2.2.6/2.2.7/2.3.0 owners, a shell-only
timeout can trigger **one post-stop persisted-history snapshot**.  Two
complete validated observations must have shown no foreground activity
and only shell jobs.  A later real query timeout does not manufacture an
observation; a contradictory/malformed response or known non-timeout API
error invalidates the hint.  The live wait still refuses, and no live
export is authorized by that hint.

After the owner closes normally and all clients detach, oc-box verifies
and stops the same existing, ready container.  If stop intent is
unchanged, it starts that exact container once for an isolated history
reader, never a replacement or a normal owning launch.  There is no
runtime/configuration/auth refresh, vault unlock, credential forwarding,
controller publication or requested model/tool execution.  The
transition budget is 35 seconds and the snapshot budget 180 seconds,
with separate bounded cleanup.  The container is stopped again before
exit.

This conservative path requires a passive container entrypoint, no
custom Podman arguments or automatic health/restart/shell-loader hooks,
and validated identity/home mounts.  It also refuses native scheduler
rows (including paused/completed rows), unknown scheduler schemas,
unsafe stores and nonempty system OpenCode configuration.  Read-only
executable-version probes must match the captured supported release on
both sides; a binary changed during the session does not inherit that
proof.  Source and destination helpers use isolated configuration,
execution denials and disabled scheduling/warming; disabling scheduling
alone is insufficient because native restart notification handling can
still wake work.  Other failures retain the normal
pending-history/manual-sync behavior.

Snapshots retain the existing complete-inventory, checked-export,
pending-session and verified-host-import safeguards.  Native
startup/projection can maintain its database; this is not a
byte-read-only database backup.  No native job is deleted, rewritten or
declared completed.  **The original failure remains nonzero even when
snapshots are saved**, with a warning that execution was interrupted and
completion notices/final results may be missing.  Historical job rows
can remain running-looking on the next owner; the same bounded post-stop
path may save another snapshot, but does not make the underlying native
owner healthy.  A 2.3.0 reload may instead drain jobs; that does not
waive ordinary readiness or prove every shutdown is quiet.  Its native
recovery regression uses an actual held background shell, live timeout
and stopped producer, not a relabeled older cancelled-job case.  Unknown
native versions retain the ordinary timeout/manual-sync behavior.  This
candidate path still requires fresh external-host acceptance before
stable publication.

‘--no-vault’ is accepted only when web becomes a new V2 owner; an
attached web client rejects it and never changes the owner's credential
snapshot.

4.7 terminal
============

On V2, the default connects to the active session's native persistent
shell terminal, also shown in OpenCode's TUI/web terminal panel.  It can
run concurrently with those clients.  The currently controlled running
PTY is reused (otherwise the last running PTY), or a new session
terminal is created when none exists.  It uses the session project and
the running server's environment.  ‘Ctrl-]’ detaches without ending the
shell; reattach to retain shell variables and state.  Typing ‘exit’ ends
the shell.  Connection takes interactive control and propagates terminal
dimensions; TUI/web clients can regain control in their terminal panel.
It does not forward new host credentials, refresh config/auth, or take
the exclusive lifecycle lock.  A ready active V2 owner is required;
start ‘oc-box’ or ‘oc-box web’ first.  Exit the attached client to
release its lease without stopping other clients.  Use ‘--maintenance’
for the shell described below.  On V1 the existing maintenance-shell
default is retained.

Open an interactive maintenance shell in an existing workspace container
instead of starting OpenCode.  Select the current project, ‘--project
PATH’, or ‘--workspace ID’.  Only these selectors, ‘--maintenance’ and
help are accepted.  Run from a real host terminal with TTY input/output;
piped or redirected input/output is refused.

     oc-box terminal
     oc-box terminal --project ~/devel/demo
     oc-box terminal --workspace WORKSPACE_ID

Exit the active OpenCode launcher first: maintenance holds the same
exclusive workspace lock as other lifecycle operations.  This prevents a
concurrent reset, purge, or normal launch.  It verifies the original
project identity, matching marker, container ownership, and project
mount.  It never creates a new workspace or container, reinstalls tools,
refreshes configuration/auth, calls OpenCode, or synchronizes history.
If the container is missing, use a normal launch to recreate it
explicitly.  A partially initialized but existing container can be
entered for debugging without rerunning baseline downloads.

If the container is stopped, terminal starts it and stops it again on
shell exit (including error/interrupt cleanup).  If already running
without an active launcher, it is left running.  A paused container must
be unpaused by the host operator first.  Type ‘exit’ or press Ctrl-D to
leave.  Ctrl-C interrupts shell commands; terminal sizing, input, and
resize events are relayed normally.  The command returns the maintenance
shell/Podman exit code.  The session IDs, source files, and private home
are not changed by the launcher; your own shell commands can change
them.  Packages installed in the OS layer survive stop/start but not
reset, just as when the AI installs them.

The shell runs with the container's existing user, devices, resource
limits, mounts, and privileges; terminal adds no mounts or capabilities.
Container root is still rootless on the host.  It opens Bash with
‘--noprofile --norc -i’, or ‘/bin/sh -i’ when Bash is unavailable.
Shell startup files are not loaded, and history persistence is disabled
(‘HISTFILE=/dev/null’, history sizes zero).

Unlike a normal OpenCode launch, terminal does not forward API keys,
tokens, custom ‘pass_env’, proxy variables, agent sockets, or
shell/loader hooks.  The Podman client receives only allowlisted host
runtime/storage discovery settings; its child shell starts with ‘env -i’
and a small fixed environment: the private HOME, project PWD, container
PATH, validated TERM, Bash SHELL, ‘C.UTF-8’ locale, container marker,
and history controls.  Credentials are not put in arguments, printed, or
copied into the container by this command.

This is maintenance access to the existing environment, not a second
security boundary inside it.  Previously copied auth/config files,
secrets deliberately written to disk, and environments of other
processes already in the container remain accessible under normal
container permissions.  To inspect environment variables, avoid posting
unredacted output from unrelated running processes.  Starting an
existing container also resumes its configured entrypoint unchanged;
terminal does not change create-time environment or undo earlier
exposure.

For example, after mapping an NPU in an earlier reset:

     oc-box terminal --maintenance --project ~/devel/npu
     ls -l /dev/accel/accel0
     prlimit --pid $$ --memlock
     xrt-smi examine
     exit

4.8 reset
=========

Recreate the environment's container OS, then launch OpenCode and resume
work.  Exit the active workspace launcher first.  Reset removes the
owned container and owned networks while retaining the private
home/session volume and any other workspace-owned volumes, project, Git
history, unfinished edits, session IDs, and resume metadata.  Installed
system packages and files outside the project/private home must be
recreated; running processes stop.  Baseline tools and OpenCode are
reinstalled automatically.  Normal V2 reset resolves the current global
vault or explicit credential policy before deleting the old container.
Wrong passphrase, corrupt configured vault, missing crypto support, or
missing selected credentials abort before deletion.  ‘--no-vault’ opts
out of vault delivery for this V2 reset only, not future starts.

Use reset to change distro, CPU/RAM/process limits, devices, or extra
Podman settings.  These are not live updates in oc-box, even though
Podman itself can update some limits.  No purge is required.  Native
binaries cached in the home may need reinstalling when switching
distributions, especially to/from Alpine.

     oc-box reset --project ~/devel/demo --cpus 8 --memory 16g
     oc-box reset --project ~/devel/demo --distro ubuntu
     oc-box reset --project ~/devel/demo --device /dev/accel/accel0

4.9 repair
==========

Explicitly accept the current directory identity for an existing
workspace at its original canonical path.  Use this after a filesystem
identity change or a deliberate project restore when you want to retain
the existing environment.  Only run it after checking that the directory
is the project you intend to use.  The host registry must still exist.
A matching regular ‘.oc-box-id’ is retained; if the marker is missing,
repair can restore it from that registry.  Symlink, invalid, or another
workspace's markers are never overwritten.  Repair cannot relocate a
workspace or reconstruct missing registry/session data.

Ordinary device-number reassignment after reboot does not need repair
once a stable filesystem/handle baseline has been recorded.  Old
registrations that already mismatch and lack that baseline still need
one explicit repair; matching legacy registrations are upgraded by the
next normal launch.

Exit the active launcher and stop its container first.  Repair takes the
workspace lock, checks container ownership, and refuses a running
container.  It updates the registry's directory device/inode and
supported filesystem-handle baseline, and restores a missing marker;
project files, container OS, private home, sessions, and saved settings
are retained.  It does not start or reset the container.  An already
matching identity needs no change.

     oc-box repair --project ~/devel/demo
     oc-box --project ~/devel/demo --resume

Accepts only ‘--project’ or ‘--workspace’ selectors, besides help.  With
neither, use the current directory.  If a missing marker has exactly one
registered workspace for this path, ‘--project’ selects it.  If several
records reference the path, repair refuses to choose: inspect ‘--status’
and supply ‘--workspace ID’ explicitly.  With ‘--workspace’, check the
registered path and restore its missing marker rather than using the
current directory.

4.10 purge
==========

Remove this workspace's labeled containers, owned volumes, owned
networks, resume metadata, private recovery exports, and matching
project marker.  Exit its active launcher first.  Shared base-image
caches and unrelated Podman resources are retained; cleanup does not use
a global prune.

Default purge retains project files and host archive conversations.  It
attempts a final history sync while the project and container exist.  If
that sync fails, the private volume is retained for recovery.  If the
project was already deleted, there is no final sync; the last host
archives remain.

Before removing the active registry, plain purge also retains its
verified archive JSON files and minimal workspace/project provenance
privately under ‘~/.local/state/oc-box/retired/ID/’ (or the configured
state root).  These are not active workspaces and do not appear in
list/status.  They allow a host archive that still names the old
controller directory to be imported later.  They may contain private
conversation content, just like host archives.  The explicit
‘--delete-sessions’ and ‘--all’ variants do not create this recovery
copy.

‘--delete-sessions’ additionally deletes the recorded host archive
copies, not an original host conversation imported into oc-box.  ‘--all’
additionally deletes the entire project directory.  Project deletion
requires the original directory identity and matching marker; if either
belongs to another workspace, ‘--all’ is rejected before deleting any
history or resources.  Extra externally supplied mounts/volumes are not
owned resources unless they bear this workspace's ownership label.

By default, enter the first eight characters of the workspace ID to
confirm.  ‘-y’ or ‘--yes’ skips this confirmation.  These deletion flags
apply only to purge.

     oc-box purge --project ~/devel/demo
     oc-box purge --project ~/devel/demo --delete-sessions
     oc-box purge --project ~/devel/disposable --all
     oc-box purge --workspace WORKSPACE_ID --all --yes

4.11 list
=========

List all registered workspaces with aligned headers: WORKSPACE ID,
DISTRO, PROJECT, STATUS, and SESSION ID. No Podman invocation or
container start is needed.  Missing project directories remain listed so
they can be purged by ID. STATUS describes directory identity/marker
health, not container running state: ‘present’, ‘identity changed’,
‘marker missing’, ‘marker mismatch’, ‘invalid marker’, ‘missing
directory’, ‘replaced directory’ (not a regular directory), or
‘inaccessible’.  Device-only changes with a matching recorded filesystem
ID, persistent handle, and inode remain ‘present’; status does not
rewrite the registry.  Unverified identity changes are not assumed to
mean replacement: legacy records may lack a stable baseline.  A marker
mismatch means another workspace is selected for that path.
Recovery/cleanup hints go to stderr and never delete or repair records
automatically.

SESSION ID is the original container session used for resume, or ‘none’.
Local metadata is refreshed at startup/sync; a new conversation in a
running client may not be reflected until the next sync or launch.  With
no workspaces, the headers are still printed.  The list is global, not
filtered by selectors; use ‘--status’ for a selected environment and
machine-readable output.

     oc-box list

4.12 distros
============

List supported distro names, image references, and the detected host
default.  No Podman invocation is required.  This is a fixed supported
image catalog; there is no custom-image option.  Selection is stored per
workspace.

     oc-box distros
     oc-box --project ~/devel/demo --distro fedora

4.13 status
===========

Alias of ‘--status’.  Report all registered environments unless
‘--project’ or ‘--workspace’ selects one.  A selector must refer to an
existing workspace.  Status is read-only and can run while OpenCode is
active; it does not start, stop, or execute commands inside managed
containers.  Use ‘--json’ for numeric, machine-readable output.  See
Resource reports for metric interpretation.

     oc-box status
     oc-box --status --project ~/devel/demo
     oc-box --status --json

4.14 upgrade
============

Check and update the installed oc-box launcher/modules and prebuilt
manuals to a published release.  Run on the host as your regular user;
root and container invocations are refused before downloads or setting
changes.  No GitHub token, Podman, or registered workspace is needed.
This command does not update host OpenCode, modify a source checkout,
reset a container, or roll back project, workspace, credential, or
session state.

The stable channel selects published final releases associated with
‘master’; devel selects published development prereleases associated
with ‘devel’.  Neither follows unpublished branch HEAD. A saved channel
is used when no selector is given; without a saved preference the
default is stable.  ‘--stable’ and ‘--devel’ are mutually exclusive,
upgrade-only selectors, not launch flags.  A successful explicit
selection is remembered for that installation owner.  GitHub and
individual package-managed owners keep separate preferences.  ‘--check’
previews the selection without installing or saving a preference.

Curl/source-installed copies use the fixed public GitHub repository.
Python-package copies instead delegate explicit upgrades to their
verified pipx, uv tool, or dedicated virtual-environment manager, using
releases of the same ‘oc-box’ PyPI project.  An ambiguous, system, or
user-site installation refuses with manager guidance, rather than
guessing or installing through GitHub.  Editable installs must update
their source checkout separately.  Package-managed normal launches never
perform GitHub automatic updates.  They reject ‘--auto-update on’;
‘--auto-update off’ prints package guidance and does not modify the
GitHub installation's setting.

Delegation is conservative: unsupported receipt versions/custom manager
options require manual commands.  uv receipt verification needs Python
3.11 or newer.  Plain pip does not record requested extras, so an
installation advertising extras needs manual guidance rather than
guessing which features to preserve.  Automatic targets must provide a
compatible, unyanked universal wheel.

     oc-box upgrade --check
     oc-box upgrade
     oc-box upgrade --devel --check
     oc-box upgrade --devel
     oc-box upgrade --stable
     oc-box upgrade --auto-update on
     oc-box upgrade --auto-update off

A GitHub upgrade clones full public history into a temporary directory,
pins the selected release tag's commit, verifies source identity and
channel branch membership, and runs its staged user-local installer.  A
normal upgrade retains a newer installed version rather than silently
downgrading.  An explicit channel switch may select an older release and
prints a downgrade warning.  This replaces only the program: it cannot
undo data/schema changes made by newer code.  Stop active clients and
retain suitable encrypted backups before a downgrade.  Source-checkout
updates remain a separate ‘git pull’/review task.

Stable public versions currently use ‘1.6.N’; development versions use
‘1.7.N.dev0’ with GitHub prerelease tags ‘v1.7.N.dev0’.  Both retain
full source identity in ‘oc-box --version’.  Channel and version series
are independent; stable can later move to 1.7 without becoming devel.
Only extras supported by the selected target are retained; current
stable 1.6 does not support ‘--with-tui’.

The installer stages a complete release under
‘~/.local/share/oc-box/releases/’, including license and manuals, before
switching the launcher.  New processes use the new version; active
launchers keep their original release path and lazy-load its original
modules.  Old release directories are retained and have no automatic
garbage collection.  ‘current’ and compatible top-level installed paths
point to the activated release.  An older flat installation is migrated;
its old manuals are preserved as ‘legacy-docs’.  No workspaces or
credentials are migrated/deleted.

Automatic GitHub updates are off by default and follow the saved GitHub
channel.  ‘--auto-update on’ saves ‘auto_update: true’ in the existing
host oc-box configuration, retaining settings such as ‘pass_env’.
Normal installed ‘start’ launches check before Podman/workspace setup
and, when updated, continue with the same arguments.  No background
daemon, active-session update, or automatic check for
list/status/terminal/reset/purge is introduced.  Direct source-checkout
launches skip automatic updates.  A concurrent check does not block
another normal launcher.  Network/download failures are reported and the
normal launch continues with the installed version; explicit upgrade
failures return nonzero.  ‘--auto-update on|off’ cannot be combined with
either channel selector or ‘--check’; select a channel and enable
automatic updates in separate commands.  Enabling them trusts future
releases on that channel, not signed release artifacts.

Fetch/checkout/install subprocesses have a clean, positive-allowlisted
environment.  Global/system Git config, helpers, hooks, external
protocols, provider tokens, SSH agents, and user proxy variables are not
used by the public download.  HTTPS is required; shell commands or a
custom upstream URL are not accepted.  Use manual source installation
for a reviewed alternate deployment.  Updates inside a container are
refused because its private HOME is not the host installation.  Git is
required for public updates; build tools are not.

Exact ‘v1.6.60’ needs one plain ‘oc-box upgrade’ to obtain channel
support before using the new flags.  Pre-feature 1.7 installations must
rerun the channel-aware bootstrap, since their updater may retain an
ahead-of-master build.  Neither bridge requires workspace reset or
session reimport.  See ‘docs/update-channels.md’ for migration and
publication policy.

4.15 secrets
============

Manage an optional encrypted credential vault on the host.  This command
never starts Podman or copies secrets into the development environment.
V1's legacy env-file default is unchanged.  V2 workspaces without an
explicit credential policy use this global vault first: all current
entries are delivered on each normal owning start/reset/import/web,
including the first project launch.  Use the literal ‘secrets’ command
first, followed by one action and, for set or remove, an uppercase
environment name.  Values and passphrases are accepted only through
masked controlling-terminal prompts, never argv, stdin, or environment
password settings.  Host-only; do not run it inside a development
container.

     oc-box secrets init
     oc-box secrets set PERSONAL_GITHUB_TOKEN
     oc-box secrets set BRAVE_API_KEY
     oc-box secrets list
     oc-box secrets remove OLD_TOKEN
     oc-box secrets passwd
     # Override the saved minimum for a new/changed passphrase:
     oc-box secrets passwd --min-passphrase-length 8

Actions: ‘init’ creates an empty vault after entering/confirming a
passphrase; it never overwrites an existing vault.  ‘set NAME’ unlocks,
then enters/confirms the secret value and creates or replaces that
entry.  ‘list’ unlocks and prints only sorted names, never values.
‘remove NAME’ unlocks and deletes the named entry; a missing entry
causes a clear error without changes.  ‘passwd’ unlocks with the old
passphrase and asks for a new confirmed passphrase.  No ‘get’, plaintext
dump, insecure batch password option, or automated env-file import is
provided.  ‘oc-box secrets --help’ describes the action syntax.

Input feedback shows a mask and character count for typing and pasting.
Secret value and confirmation prompts start masked and offer **Ctrl+R**
to toggle visible/masked input on the fly.  Passphrase creation,
confirmation, and unlock prompts always stay masked: Ctrl+R does not
reveal or modify passphrases.  Reveal state never carries to the next
prompt.  There is no flag or setting that makes credentials visible by
default.

Editing keys: Left/Right move, Home/End (or Ctrl+A/Ctrl+E) select the
ends, Backspace/Delete remove a character, Ctrl+U clears the entire
value, Ctrl+W removes the preceding word, and Ctrl+K clears from the
cursor to the end.  Enter accepts; Ctrl+C, Ctrl+D, or Ctrl+Z cancels
without changing the vault.  Ctrl+Z cancels rather than suspending a
process that is holding sensitive input.  The normal terminal settings
and bracketed-paste mode are restored on exit/errors.

Long values use a width-bounded horizontal viewport with a count, so
they do not normally wrap; only the current field is displayed, never
prior vault values.  UTF-8 input is preserved exactly, with byte limits
applied before insertion.  Terminal control/bidi/combining characters
are never executed/rendered as active terminal sequences when revealed.
Bracketed paste treats its contents as data, not toggle/edit/submit
keystrokes: multiline, control-character, invalid UTF-8, or oversized
bracketed pastes are rejected as a whole.  Correct/reenter rejected
input before submission (Ctrl+U clears it); it is not silently
truncated.  A terminal without bracketed-paste support cannot
distinguish a pasted newline from Enter, so paste only a single-line
token with no trailing newline.

The field is erased on completion/cancellation, including when revealed.
This does not undo exposure to terminal recordings, screen
capture/sharing, or scrollback/reflow.  A visible value is an
intentional display of a secret on your host terminal, not a way to
export or log it; mask/count feedback also discloses length.  No prompt
input is written to stdout/stderr, shell history, registry, or plaintext
temporary files, and the encrypted vault format is unchanged.

Optional cryptography support is required only when using vault
operations:

     pipx install 'oc-box[vault]'
     # Add support to an existing pipx installation:
     pipx inject oc-box 'cryptography>=44'
     # Or install/reinstall the uv tool with its extra:
     uv tool install --force 'oc-box[vault]'
     # In a package installation's activated virtual environment:
     python -m pip install 'oc-box[vault]'

For a fresh V2 installation, choose the ‘oc-box[vault]’ extra (or the
curl option below), then run ‘oc-box secrets init’ and ‘oc-box secrets
set NAME’ **before your first** ‘oc-box --project ~/devel/demo’.  Global
use needs no per-project registration or selection.  Values are entered
only in masked prompts; do not put secret values in OpenCode or oc-box
JSON.  Configuration can reference their names using the published V2
shapes under Configuration and environment.

For GitHub/source installations, ‘python3 install.py --with-vault’
stages the optional crypto wheels privately in that release (using a
temporary venv), not in system/user-site Python.  The curl bootstrap
accepts ‘sh -s -- --with-vault’ when piped to a shell.  Future GitHub
upgrades preserve that opt-in.  The source can also use a host Python
with a distro-provided cryptography package.  A missing dependency
yields guidance, not a fallback to weak/custom encryption.  No desktop
keyring, external password manager, cloud service, or daemon is used.
With no host vault, normal default V2 launches retain the env-file
fallback and suggest setup; they never install crypto, initialize a
vault, edit config, or import JSON secrets automatically.  An
initialized empty vault still unlocks but supplies no vault entries;
guidance suggests ‘oc-box secrets set NAME’.  A present corrupt vault,
wrong passphrase, or missing crypto support aborts before container
setup/reset deletion, with no silent fallback.  Native OpenCode
saved/OAuth credential refresh and copying remain independent in every
credential mode, including global vault mode: env entries do not replace
structured OAuth refresh-token records.  A vault containing only
‘BRAVE_API_KEY’ does not disable existing Copilot OAuth.  Configured
global mode suppresses legacy ambient token/env overrides, including
‘OPENCODE_AUTH_CONTENT’, but keeps ordinary supported native auth
integration.  No plaintext OpenCode JSON secrets are imported or
rewritten into the vault.

AES-256-GCM authenticates the ciphertext and format/KDF metadata; Scrypt
uses a random 16-byte salt with n=131072, r=8, p=1 (approximately 128
MiB memory) and a 32-byte key.  Each write generates a fresh salt and
12-byte nonce.  Crypto is implemented by the maintained cryptography
library, not handwritten primitives.  Format version 1 accepts only
those bounded parameters; tampered costs cannot trigger arbitrary memory
allocation.  New/changed passphrases default to a minimum of 8
characters, configurable with ‘vault_min_passphrase_length’ in the host
oc-box config or ‘--min-passphrase-length’ on ‘init’/‘passwd’.  Integers
from 8 to 1024 are accepted; longer passwords are allowed up to 1024
UTF-8 bytes.  Existing passphrases unlock regardless of the current
creation minimum.  Entries are limited to 128, each nonempty UTF-8 value
at most 64 KiB with no NUL, and the complete encrypted file to 2 MiB.
Names, unlike workspace selections, are encrypted too.

The host vault normally resides at
‘~/.local/share/oc-box/credentials/vault.json’, or its XDG_DATA_HOME
equivalent.  Its directory must be owned by your user with 0700 and the
regular vault file with private 0600 permissions.  Symlink paths, extra
hard links, wrong ownership, insecure permissions, invalid formats, and
nonregular vault/lock files are rejected.  Files are locked during
read-modify-write.  Ciphertext is written to a 0600 temporary file,
fsynced, and atomically replaced; no plaintext temp file or key file is
created.  Do not locate the vault inside a mounted project.

There is no passphrase recovery or permanent unlock/cache agent.
Forgotten passphrases make the encrypted data unrecoverable.  Back up
ciphertext and store the passphrase separately.  Removing an entry or
changing a passphrase does not erase older backups/snapshots or revoke
the provider token.  WSL requires a host terminal and filesystem
permissions such as its Linux home filesystem; this is not a promise
that an arbitrary Windows/shared mount enforces Unix permissions.
Python memory is not guaranteed zeroized; core dumps are disabled on
secret prompt entry where permitted, but host debugging/swap/hardware
compromise and secrets delivered to container processes are outside
at-rest encryption.  During vault-enabled launches and explicit private
captures (including V2 native saved/OAuth credential transfer with
‘--no-vault’), host captured subprocess output uses anonymous Linux
memfd storage rather than disk-backed temporary files.
Launcher-generated failure messages redact exact active vault values.
This is not a general redaction filter for OpenCode, provider logs, tool
output, or session archives: an agent can still print, save, or transmit
a credential it has received.

4.16 credentials
================

View or change **names-only** forwarding policy for an existing
workspace.  Accepted modes are ‘env’, ‘selected’, and ‘vault’.  Without
an explicit saved policy, V1 defaults to legacy ‘env’; V2 defaults to
global vault-first, including existing workspaces that have no policy.
Existing explicit env/selected policies remain intentional overrides; an
upgrade does not replace them.  With no selection options, print mode
and selected names without unlocking the vault, revealing values,
starting a container, or refreshing config/auth.

     oc-box credentials --project ~/devel/demo
     oc-box credentials --workspace WORKSPACE_ID --credential-mode selected \
       --env-secret GITHUB_TOKEN --vault-secret PERSONAL_GITHUB_TOKEN

Requires the registered project/marker and exclusive workspace lock.
Exit its launcher before configuration; changes take effect on the next
launch without reset or session import.  Selectors default to the
current directory; no new workspace is created here.  Start a new
environment normally once to register it, exit, then configure
**overrides** if needed.  Default global vault use already works on that
first V2 launch without this command.  Use only selectors and credential
options; session, device, resource, deletion, or update options are
refused.

In ‘vault’ mode on V2, all current global encrypted entries are selected
dynamically each time a new owner starts.  No automatic list of names is
frozen or saved in the registry.  Adding/removing entries affects the
next new owner, not a running service or attached clients.  When a host
vault is present, global mode replaces ambient automatic
‘_TOKEN’/‘_API_KEY’ and custom ‘pass_env’ forwarding; fixed runtime and
noncredential routing preferences remain.  Global delivery is limited to
1 MiB of combined UTF-8 environment names/values including separators.
Larger valid vaults remain stored unchanged, but a global launch fails
before setup/reset deletion; use selected mode with fewer entries.
Nonempty per-name env/vault selections are refused in this mode.  To
switch an existing selection to global use, clear it explicitly:

     oc-box credentials --project ~/devel/demo --credential-mode vault --clear-credentials
     oc-box --project ~/devel/demo

In ‘selected’ mode, only the explicitly selected env/vault names are
delivered, plus the launcher's fixed runtime settings and noncredential
language/color/ model-routing preferences.  This replaces automatic
suffix forwarding and global ‘pass_env’, not just supplements them.
Vault and env names cannot overlap.  Uppercase credential names cannot
override loader/shell/host runtime settings such as PATH, BASH_ENV,
LD_PRELOAD, OPENCODE_AUTH_CONTENT, or OC_BOX variables.  Names
append/deduplicate; ‘--clear-credentials’ clears both lists before
adding.

     oc-box credentials --project ~/devel/demo --credential-mode selected \
       --clear-credentials --vault-secret PERSONAL_GITHUB_TOKEN --vault-secret BRAVE_API_KEY
     oc-box --project ~/devel/demo
     oc-box credentials --project ~/devel/demo --credential-mode env --clear-credentials

Explicit ‘env’ mode retains legacy automatic suffix/pass_env forwarding.
It may also supplement inherited variables with selected vault names; a
vault value then wins over an inherited same-named env value.  Saved
‘vault’ mode with runtime V1 uses legacy env forwarding without
unlocking all-global entries; explicit old env/selected vault selections
continue to work as before.

A normal owning start/reset/import/web unlocks once when using a present
vault in global mode or when selected vault names exist.  The decrypted
values stay only in the launcher's memory/context and private container
process environments.  They are not written to the workspace metadata,
host os.environ, command arguments, config files, or a cache.  Values
reach the container through Podman's inherited env names; the vault and
passphrase are never mounted.  Wrong passphrase, corrupt configured
vault, missing crypto support, or missing selection aborts before
container setup/reset deletion.  No vault present permits the
default/global V2 env-file fallback; an initialized empty vault unlocks
and delivers no vault entries, with ‘secrets set NAME’ guidance.

V2-only ‘--no-vault’ on owning start/reset/web skips vault entries once
without saving policy: global mode uses legacy env forwarding for that
invocation; explicit env/selected modes retain their env constraints.
It is rejected on attached clients and all unrelated commands.  Attach
uses the already-running service and never unlocks or refreshes
credentials.

Informational commands, maintenance terminal, explicit history sync,
stop, repair, and upgrade never unlock.  The maintenance shell's clean
environment rules remain unchanged.  Configuration/auth copying is an
independent feature: these selections do not remove embedded credentials
in copied config, provider or MCP auth files, project files, raw Podman
create-time Env settings, previous processes, or previously saved
container data.  Selection is reduced forwarding, not an isolation
boundary from secrets already inside the container.  Delivered keys
remain readable by the agent/tools with normal container permissions.

4.17 backup
===========

Create one encrypted ‘.ocbox’ file for an existing workspace (available
since 1.0.0).  Requires host age/age-keygen, Python, rootless Podman,
and Linux memfd support; it does not require the vault crypto extra or
unlock the credential vault.  The command must be first, with its own
options after it.  Use -project/-workspace or the current directory.

     oc-box backup --project ~/devel/demo --output /backup/demo.ocbox
     oc-box backup --workspace WORKSPACE_ID --portable --output /backup/demo-portable.ocbox

The workspace lock requires its active OpenCode launcher or maintenance
shell to exit.  The command verifies marker/physical identity and
resource ownership, then stops the container if needed; it remains
stopped afterward.  Host editors, builds, and other processes must not
change project files during capture.  The launcher lock cannot quiesce
unrelated host writers.  Project and container configuration must be
intact; missing containers and unknown extra bind mounts/ volumes are
refused rather than silently omit outside data.

Full backup (default) captures the complete project including ‘.git’,
untracked files and unfinished edits; the private home with the live
OpenCode SQLite DB, subagent transcripts, snapshots, copied config/auth,
and caches; registry/resume evidence; and a committed OCI image of the
modified container OS. Mounted project and home are backed up
separately, not folded into the image.  Portable mode omits that OS
image; system packages/SDKs must be recreated after restore.  Neither
mode captures running processes, kernel state, devices, host drivers,
the host-wide vault, host global DB/config, or arbitrary paths outside
those components.  Env files inside the selected project are project
data and are included; host env files outside it are not read.
Workspace credential modes/policies are retained, but the global host
vault is excluded: protect its encrypted file and passphrase separately.
A full workspace backup can include config/auth values already persisted
inside the workspace, even though normal vault forwarding itself does
not write them to config.

Default encryption asks for a new/confirmed masked backup passphrase
(default minimum 8 characters), separate from the vault password.  Set
‘backup_min_passphrase_length’ in host oc-box config or use
‘--min-passphrase-length N’ for this backup.  Both accept integers from
8 to 1024; longer passwords are allowed up to 1024 UTF-8 bytes.
Blank/short/mismatched input is rejected, rather than asking age to
generate a password.  oc-box passes the validated password to standard
age through a private, echo-disabled terminal; no password enters argv,
environment variables, or plaintext files.  Age still provides the
cipher/KDF and prompts directly when unlocking existing backups, which
are not restricted by the current creation minimum.  For unattended
encryption use a native age X25519 public recipient, repeatable:

     age-keygen -o ~/.config/backup-age-key.txt
     age-keygen -y ~/.config/backup-age-key.txt
     oc-box backup --project ~/devel/demo --recipient age1PUBLIC_KEY \
       --output /backup/demo.ocbox

The generated private key file is sensitive and should remain 0600
outside project/container access.  The public recipient may be stored in
a scheduler.  Keep the private identity/backup passphrase separately;
losing it makes the backup unrecoverable.  Age plugins/SSH identities
are not accepted in format v1.

The envelope contains fixed-name age-encrypted components plus an
encrypted manifest and age-protected per-backup private key.  The
manifest authenticates component SHA-256 digests/sizes; all components
use age authenticated streaming encryption.  Plaintext streams through
pipes, and secret keys/captured stderr use anonymous memory.  Temporary
backup staging contains ciphertext only.  At most 16 TiB
ciphertext/expanded archive bytes per component and 2 million entries
are accepted in format v1.  The output parent must exist outside the
project, registry, and private home.  New output is published atomically
without replacing an existing file and has 0600 permissions.  Expect
enough free space for staged ciphertext, final output, and a temporary
committed image.  Failure leaves no final partial backup; the source
remains stopped, not reset/deleted.  The temporary capture image is
removed by its unique tag; shared base images remain.

4.18 backup-info
================

Decrypt/verify a backup's manifest and ciphertext checksums without
starting Podman or extracting workspace contents.  Requires age and a
controlling terminal for passphrase backups, or a native private
-identity file for recipient backups.  Output is JSON metadata including
kind, source path/ID, architecture, component sizes/checksums, saved
session IDs, settings, and documented limitations.  Raw additional
Podman option values are omitted to avoid printing saved secrets.  It
never prints archived file contents or the host vault and does not
execute container image code.  Metadata itself may contain private
paths/session names.

     oc-box backup-info /backup/demo.ocbox
     oc-box backup-info /backup/demo.ocbox --identity ~/.config/backup-age-key.txt

Inspection stages only encrypted envelope components while checking
them.  It authenticates the manifest and binds component ciphertext to
it; restore also verifies every age data stream through its final
authentication chunk.  Wrong keys, corruption, missing/extra components,
unsupported versions, or unsafe outer envelope paths are rejected before
restore activation.

4.19 restore
============

Streaming failures identify the producer/consumer exit code and a fixed
category (for example exhausted storage, permission denial or archive
rejection); private tool stderr is never printed.  Full image load also
needs Podman's host temporary image space (normally ‘/var/tmp’), in
addition to destination image/private-home storage.  Backup/restore
subprocesses honor an explicit ‘TMPDIR’ so host temporary image files
and encrypted-component staging can use a suitable filesystem:

     TMPDIR=/var/tmp oc-box restore /backup/demo.ocbox --project ~/devel/demo-restored \
       --identity ~/.config/backup-age-key.txt

The directory must exist and be usable by your regular user.  This
changes temporary placement, not Podman's image-store location or its
storage quotas.  Filesystem-wide free bytes do not establish
user/project quota headroom.  Keep the encrypted source archive after
failure and use a new absent destination after incomplete-restore
cleanup; no failed staging is activated.

Restore into a **new, nonexistent** host project destination.  Never
overwrites an existing directory or workspace; there is no
-force/-replace mode.  Run on the host as your regular user after
installing age.  The active source workspace does not need to be
removed: this is a fresh independent environment.

     oc-box restore /backup/demo.ocbox --project ~/devel/demo-restored
     oc-box restore /backup/demo.ocbox --project ~/devel/demo-restored \
       --identity ~/.config/backup-age-key.txt

It decrypts/verifies the manifest, checks architecture/settings, stages
project files in a private sibling directory, creates a fresh labeled
home volume, and streams its extraction under podman unshare for
container UID/GID mappings.  Registry content is retained as
‘recovered-registry/’ evidence, not activated as arbitrary create
commands or old host-archive paths.  Then a full backup loads the
captured OCI image; portable mode selects the original distro catalog
image.  Only after component restoration succeeds does atomic no-replace
rename publish the project and record its actual destination identity
with fresh IDs.

The new environment is **created but not started**.  Its resume
selection keeps the original conversation IDs and complete container DB.
Original private home and in-container project paths remain the same;
‘container_project’ in metadata can differ from the new host project.
Existing session snapshots/tool paths need no database rewrite.  Status
reports both paths when different.  Host archive sync from a restored
workspace uses fresh namespaced archive IDs and the new host project.
Old host archive associations are not reused/deleted.

Normal startup still copies the current host config/auth and checks the
exact host OpenCode version.  The archive's device/extra Podman settings
are recorded as requirements but **not automatically activated**.  The
restored image's environment and declared volumes are cleared, image
healthchecks are disabled, and a fixed entrypoint is used; backup image
hooks, startup commands, external mounts, and host device settings are
not trusted as new host launch privileges.  Full restores retain OS
files, which are executable code when you later start/use that
environment: restore only trusted backups.  Portable restores recreate
baseline tools on first normal launch and need custom system
dependencies reinstalled.  Host drivers and CPU architecture are not
portable; this initial format requires a matching CPU architecture even
for portable mode because cached home binaries may be native.

Extraction refuses traversal, duplicate/excessive entries, special
devices/ FIFOs/sockets, sparse extensions, oversized metadata, hardlinks
outside earlier regular entries, and any write through a symlink
ancestor.  Project files become owned by the current host user;
private-home ownership is mapped in the rootless namespace.  Regular
modes/timestamps/symlinks/hardlinks are kept, but setuid/setgid/ sticky
bits, ACLs, xattrs, and source SELinux labels are not restored.  New
project mounts use Podman's normal :z relabeling.  Symlink targets may
name outside paths but are never followed during extraction; check them
before later host use.  Restored project and staging roots are kept
private (0700), even if the original root had broader permissions;
permissions of ordinary files/subdirectories remain.

The global host vault is not included or unlocked.  Workspace credential
modes/policies are retained, with any selections stored as names only.
A restored default/global V2 workspace uses the **restoring host's**
current vault, not the original host's vault or old host privileges.
Provide matching host env/vault credentials for an explicit
selected-mode policy before normal startup.  Outside env files are
excluded; back up the encrypted host vault separately.  Copied
auth/config and secrets stored in project/home/OS are part of the
encrypted payload and remain present after restore.  A failed operation
cleans only its newly created project/volume/container/registry and
preserves original environments; interruption may retain staging marked
incomplete, which normal launch refuses until the operator
inspects/retries.  Loaded OCI images may remain in shared Podman image
cache after failure; no global prune is used.  The host registry
journals fresh resource IDs and ‘restore_staging_project’ before payload
extraction.  An interrupted or failed-cleanup entry has
‘restore_incomplete: true’; list/status can expose its ID without
launching it.  Inspect the staging location, use plain ‘purge
--workspace ID’ for its managed resources (which skips history sync for
incomplete restores), and retry to a new destination.  Orphan staging
directories may need manual host removal after inspection; the launcher
never deletes a caller-supplied tree on marker alone.


