5 Options
*********

5.1 -h, -help
=============

Print command choices, options, and short descriptions; exit without
Podman.  For example: ‘oc-box --help’.

5.2 -min-passphrase-length N
============================

Set the minimum character count for a new vault or backup passphrase for
this operation.  Accepted only with ‘oc-box secrets init’, ‘oc-box
secrets passwd’, and ‘oc-box backup’; it is incompatible with backup
‘--recipient’.  N must be an integer from 8 to 1024.  The default is 8
unless the corresponding host config key ‘vault_min_passphrase_length’
or ‘backup_min_passphrase_length’ is set; this flag overrides that key
without saving it.  Passwords may exceed the minimum, subject to the
1024 UTF-8-byte limit.  Unlock/restore never enforces a new creation
policy against an existing password.  The number is public policy, not a
way to pass a password in argv.  For example:

     oc-box secrets init --min-passphrase-length 8
     oc-box secrets passwd --min-passphrase-length 16
     oc-box backup --project ~/devel/demo --output /backup/demo.ocbox \
       --min-passphrase-length 12

5.3 -feature FEATURE
====================

Doctor-only, repeatable.  Limit the dependency report and exit-status
evaluation to named features.  ‘oc-box doctor --feature backup-info’
checks age and memory support without requiring Podman or host OpenCode.
With no selector, report all features and evaluate launch prerequisites
for the exit status.  Use ‘oc-box doctor --help’ for the complete
accepted names.

5.4 -maintenance
================

Terminal-only.  Open the restricted maintenance shell instead of a live
V2 session client.  This retains the existing allowlisted environment,
no host credential forwarding/unlock, no config/auth refresh, no startup
profiles and no persistent shell history.  Maintenance holds the
exclusive workspace lock; exit the active owner first.  Without this
flag, terminal joins an active V2 session's actual persistent shell PTY;
1.x continues to use the existing maintenance shell.

5.5 -port N
===========

Web-only.  Select the host-loopback relay port: 0 (default) chooses a
free port; explicit ports are 1..65535.  Binding failure is reported
without replacing an existing listener.  The container service's own
random loopback port stays private.

5.6 -no-open
============

Web-only.  Print the native one-use browser pairing link instead of
invoking the host browser.  The foreground relay still runs until Ctrl-C
or workspace stop.

5.7 -project PATH
=================

Select an existing project directory; relative paths resolve from the
host working directory.  Quote paths containing spaces.  For lifecycle
commands the default is the current directory.  For status, omission
means all workspaces.  The project must not be the host home, ‘/’,
‘/home’, the oc-box registry, or a directory containing trusted
launcher/state files.  Colons, commas, and line breaks in the project
path are unsupported because of Podman mount syntax.

     oc-box --project "$HOME/devel/my project"

5.8 -workspace ID
=================

Select an existing workspace by its full 32-character ID from ‘oc-box
list’.  This works from another directory and allows cleanup after the
project is deleted.  Starting, syncing, or resetting still needs its
original project directory.  The identity marker and registry are not
interchangeable with OpenCode session IDs.  Example: ‘oc-box --status
--workspace WORKSPACE_ID’.

5.9 -status
===========

Report CPU, RAM, storage, state, limits, and sessions without opening
OpenCode.  Equivalent to ‘status’.  Only selectors and ‘--json’ apply.
It cannot be combined with another command, session mode, resource
change, or deletion action.

5.10 -json
==========

Doctor also accepts this flag to emit its versioned feature/dependency
report.

Emit status as one JSON object with ‘sampled_at’ and ‘workspaces’.
Sizes are numeric bytes and unavailable values are ‘null’; notes explain
partial results.  Progress goes to stderr, not the JSON stream.  Status
uses this schema; doctor uses the feature/dependency schema described
under that command.

     oc-box --status --json > status.json

5.11 -about
===========

Print oc-box version, author Paulo Dias and email, OpenAI
coding-assistant contribution, GPL-3.0-or-later licensing, and source
commit.  No Podman or workspace is required.  Use alone, without
lifecycle selectors/options.

5.12 -version
=============

Print oc-box's commit-derived version and exit without Podman.  Current
stable format is ‘1.6.N+gHASH’; development format is
‘1.7.N.dev0+gHASH’, with N the full-history commit count minus 29 (the
original 1.0.0 baseline).  Each later commit advances N; shallow history
cannot supply a release identity.  Tracked uncommitted changes add
‘.dirty’.  Untracked files do not.  Installed copies retain their
installation-time identity in ‘build-info.json’; reinstall to update it.
This is separate from the host OpenCode version matched inside
containers.  Use alone, for example: ‘oc-box --version’.

The OpenCode V2-compatible 1.5 series and the 1.6 refactoring series
retain the same commit-derived patch count; a first published patch is
not required to be zero.  Installed older copies keep their original
0.1/1.0/1.5 identity.  PyPI distribution metadata uses ‘1.6.N’ or
‘1.7.N.dev0’, because public PyPI versions cannot contain the local
‘+gHASH’ suffix.  Its packaged build-info still includes the full source
identity displayed by this command, so the exact Git commit is retained
even in a wheel/source distribution installed without Git.  Existing
0.1/1.0 installations retain their original embedded identity until
upgraded through their usual channel.  The absolute commit count remains
in metadata for older-updater compatibility; no workspace reset or
database migration is required for the version-series transition.

5.13 -distro NAME
=================

Choose a supported distro for a new environment.  The default matches
the host ID when supported; openSUSE Tumbleweed/Slowroll use Tumbleweed,
other openSUSE variants use Leap, and unknown hosts fall back to
Tumbleweed.  The image is a distribution userland, not an exact clone of
host packages or snapshot date.  Changing an existing workspace requires
‘reset --distro NAME’.

     oc-box --project ~/devel/demo --distro tumbleweed
     oc-box reset --project ~/devel/demo --distro debian

5.14 -check
===========

With ‘upgrade’, preview the selected published release without
installing it or changing the saved channel.  May be combined with
‘--stable’ or ‘--devel’.  Returns success when a version is available or
the installation is current; it is not an update-available exit code.
Network errors return nonzero.  Cannot be combined with ‘--auto-update’,
workspace selectors, or other commands.  Example: ‘oc-box upgrade
--check’.

5.15 -stable
============

With ‘upgrade’, explicitly select published stable releases from
‘master’ (not unpublished branch HEAD). A successful selection is saved
for that installation owner unless ‘--check’ is given.  Mutually
exclusive with ‘--devel’ and ‘--auto-update on|off’; rejected on every
other command, including implicit start.  An explicit switch to an older
stable release warns about the downgrade and does not roll back
workspace/session state.  Example: ‘oc-box upgrade --stable’.

5.16 -devel
===========

With ‘upgrade’, explicitly select published development prereleases from
‘devel’, not arbitrary development commits.  A successful selection is
saved for that installation owner unless ‘--check’ is given.  Uses the
same PyPI project for package-managed installations.  Mutually exclusive
with ‘--stable’ and ‘--auto-update on|off’; rejected on every other
command, including implicit start.  Example: ‘oc-box upgrade --devel
--check’.

5.17 -auto-update on|off
========================

With ‘upgrade’, persistently enable or disable automatic updates before
normal installed launches.  The default is off.  This setting itself
performs no download or installation and preserves other keys in
‘~/.config/oc-box/config.json’ (or its XDG equivalent).  Follows the
saved GitHub release channel; it does not select one.  Cannot be
combined with ‘--check’, ‘--stable’, ‘--devel’ or workspace actions.
Package-managed installations reject ‘on’; ‘off’ gives manager guidance
without changing GitHub settings.  Example: ‘oc-box upgrade
--auto-update on’.

5.18 -new
=========

Start a new root conversation while retaining the same container and
files.  Use with start (the default) or reset.  Mutually exclusive with
‘--resume’ and ‘--import-session’.  Example: ‘oc-box --project
~/devel/demo --new’.

5.19 -resume
============

Explicitly request the normal resume behavior.  Requires a registered
workspace; if one exists but no root session has been recorded, OpenCode
can start a new conversation.  Use with start/reset.  Mutually exclusive
with ‘--new’ and ‘--import-session’.  Example: ‘oc-box --project
~/devel/demo --resume’.

5.20 -import-session SESSION_ID
===============================

Copy a host conversation from the selected project into the private
container database, verify the transcript, select the exact ID, and
launch it.  Start-only; mutually exclusive with ‘--new’ and ‘--resume’.
Quit the source host client first to include its final saved turn.  A
subagent session cannot be imported as a root.  The original host
conversation is retained.  See Sessions and recovery.  An oc-box host
archive can also be imported into a fresh environment for that same
project; the original registry and saved archive verify its project.

     oc-box --project ~/devel/demo --import-session ses_HOST_SESSION_ID

5.21 -model PROVIDER/MODEL
==========================

Pass the provider/model choice to the OpenCode TUI on this launch.  It
is not a persistent oc-box workspace setting.  Use with start/reset;
otherwise normal OpenCode provider/model preferences apply.

     oc-box --project ~/devel/demo --model github-copilot/gpt-6.1-sol

5.22 -credential-mode env|selected|vault
========================================

With ‘credentials’, save an explicit forwarding mode for that workspace.
‘env’ retains legacy suffix/pass_env forwarding, optionally supplemented
with selected vault names.  ‘selected’ forwards only configured
credential names, plus fixed runtime/nonsecret routing settings.
‘vault’ uses all current global entries on each new V2 owner, not a
frozen name list; nonempty per-name selections are refused.  Without a
saved policy, V1 defaults to env and V2 to global vault-first.  Explicit
env/selected policies remain overrides.  V1 with saved vault mode uses
legacy env forwarding without all-global unlock.  Changes apply on the
next owner, not to an active client.  To replace an existing selection:

     oc-box credentials --project ~/devel/demo --credential-mode vault --clear-credentials

5.23 -no-vault
==============

V2-only, for one **owning** start (including ‘--import-session’), reset,
or web invocation.  Skip vault use without changing the saved workspace
policy.  Global mode temporarily uses legacy env-file forwarding;
explicit env/selected modes skip vault entries but retain their env
constraints.  The next normal owner uses the saved/default policy again.
Attached TUI/web clients reject this flag and never unlock or refresh an
existing service.  V1, info/status/list/doctor, credentials/secrets,
terminal/maintenance, sync, stop, repair, purge, backup/info/ restore,
upgrade, and other non-owning commands reject it.  It does not erase
already persisted config/auth or hide secrets from existing container
processes.

     oc-box start --project ~/devel/demo --no-vault
     oc-box reset --project ~/devel/demo --no-vault
     oc-box web --project ~/devel/demo --no-vault --no-open

5.24 -env-secret NAME
=====================

With ‘credentials’, append an uppercase environment credential name to
the workspace's explicit env selection, repeatable and deduplicated.  In
selected mode it must be present/nonempty in the sourced host
environment before launch; a missing value aborts before unlock/setup.
Env mode already forwards legacy variables, so this list becomes
restrictive only in selected mode.  No value is accepted here.  Nonempty
per-name selections are refused in vault mode; choose an explicit
env/selected mode to use them.  Example: ‘oc-box credentials
--credential-mode selected --env-secret PERSONAL_GITHUB_TOKEN’.

5.25 -vault-secret NAME
=======================

With ‘credentials’, select an existing encrypted vault entry name to
unlock and forward on normal owning start/reset/import/web launches.
Repeat for multiple names.  The name is saved in workspace metadata,
never its secret value.  Vault presence is checked when unlocking, not
by this names-only configuration command.  A name cannot also be
selected with -env-secret.  Example: ‘oc-box credentials
--credential-mode selected --vault-secret BRAVE_API_KEY’.  Vault mode
instead uses every current entry and refuses nonempty per-name lists.

5.26 -clear-credentials
=======================

With ‘credentials’, clear both saved env-name and vault-name lists
before adding new selections on this invocation.  It does not erase the
vault, remove provider auth/config, revoke credentials, or change mode
unless -credential-mode is also specified.  Example: ‘oc-box credentials
--credential-mode env --clear-credentials’ returns to the original env
workflow without deleting encrypted vault entries.  Use
‘--credential-mode vault --clear-credentials’ to replace selections with
dynamic global V2 use.  Clearing alone does not change the mode.

5.27 -output FILE
=================

Backup-only.  Required new encrypted backup output pathname.  Its parent
must already exist outside the project/registry/private volume.
Existing files or symlinks are never overwritten, including a
destination created during capture.  Example: ‘oc-box backup --project
~/devel/demo --output /backup/demo.ocbox’.  The archive suffix is
conventional; the versioned format is checked on restore.

5.28 -portable
==============

Backup-only.  Omit the container OS image while retaining project,
complete private home, sessions, registry evidence, and
limits/credential-name settings.  Restore rebuilds the selected distro
OS/baseline tools; custom system packages and native-cache compatibility
are the user's responsibility.  Example: ‘oc-box backup --project
~/devel/demo --portable --output /backup/demo.ocbox’.

5.29 -recipient AGE_KEY
=======================

Backup-only, repeatable (up to 16).  Encrypt the backup key envelope to
a native age X25519 public key, allowing backup without a password
prompt.  Any matching private identity can decrypt it.  Without this
option age requests a backup passphrase interactively.  Public
recipients are not secret; private identities must be stored
independently of the archive and project.  Example: ‘oc-box backup
--recipient age1PUBLIC_KEY --output /backup/demo.ocbox’.

5.30 -identity FILE
===================

Restore/backup-info only, repeatable.  Read a native age X25519 private
identity file owned by the host user with private permissions, no
symlink or extra hard links.  Plugins, SSH identities, and encrypted
identity files are refused in the initial format; passphrase-encrypted
backups instead use age's terminal prompt.  No identity text or
passphrase is accepted through argv/environment.  Example: ‘oc-box
backup-info /backup/demo.ocbox --identity ~/.config/backup-age-key.txt’.

5.31 -cpus NUMBER
=================

Set the CPU quota in core units; fractions such as ‘2.5’ are accepted by
Podman.  Default: ‘4’.  Set at creation or reset.  Existing containers,
even stopped ones, require reset to change it; oc-box does not call
‘podman update’.  Values are validated by Podman.  ‘0’ requests no CPU
quota where supported.

     oc-box reset --project ~/devel/demo --cpus 8

5.32 -memory SIZE
=================

Set the memory limit using Podman size syntax, such as ‘512m’, ‘8g’, or
‘16g’.  Default: ‘8g’.  Set at creation or reset; not a live update.
The value is saved for future launches.  Limits require delegated cgroup
controllers.  Podman validates the size and kernel support.  ‘0’
requests no memory limit where supported.  There is no automatic
project/volume disk quota.

     oc-box reset --project ~/devel/demo --memory 16g

5.33 -pids-limit NUMBER
=======================

Set the container's process limit; default ‘2048’.  oc-box requires at
least ‘32’.  Set at creation or reset; changing an existing container
requires reset.  Example: ‘oc-box reset --project ~/devel/demo
--pids-limit 4096’.

5.34 -device MAPPING
====================

Add a persistent Podman mapping, repeatable.  Supports
‘/host/device[:/container/device][:rwm]’ and CDI ‘vendor/class=name’.
New settings append to saved mappings; identical strings are
deduplicated.  Changing existing mappings requires reset.  Use
‘--clear-devices’ to replace or remove them.  Host permissions, SELinux,
and installed driver userland still apply; device mapping alone does not
install an NPU/GPU SDK.

     oc-box reset --project ~/devel/demo --device /dev/accel/accel0
     oc-box --project ~/devel/gpu --device /dev/dri/renderD128:rw

5.35 -podman-arg=-OPTION[=VALUE]
================================

Append one literal long-option token to the Podman create command before
the image.  Repeat the flag for multiple options.  Use ‘=’ both after
‘--podman-arg’ and within valued Podman options; this is not a shell
command string or a space-split list.  Values containing spaces remain
one argument when quoted.  Saved options persist across launches/resets;
identical strings are deduplicated.  Remove old/conflicting options with
‘--clear-podman-args’ when replacing them.

The following are reserved by oc-box: ‘--name’, ‘--label’,
‘--label-file’, ‘--cidfile’, ‘--replace’, ‘--rm’, ‘--entrypoint’,
‘--workdir’, ‘--user’, ‘--rootfs’, ‘--pod’, and ‘--pod-id-file’.  Use
oc-box's dedicated CPU, memory, and process flags instead of forwarding
their Podman equivalents.  Options are for ‘podman create’, not global
Podman flags or arguments after the image.

     oc-box reset --project ~/devel/demo \
       --podman-arg=--shm-size=1g \
       --podman-arg=--ulimit=memlock=-1:-1

Extra devices, mounts, capabilities, host networking, and security
options extend the default boundary as requested by the host operator.
Podman and the host's rootless permissions determine which settings are
available.

5.36 -clear-devices
===================

Clear all saved ‘--device’ mappings before adding any new ones on this
command.  Use during reset for an existing container.  It does not
remove a device passed as a raw Podman option; use ‘--clear-podman-args’
for that list.

     oc-box reset --project ~/devel/demo --clear-devices
     oc-box reset --project ~/devel/demo --clear-devices --device /dev/accel/accel1

5.37 -clear-podman-args
=======================

Clear saved additional Podman options before appending replacements.
Use during reset for an existing container.  It leaves the dedicated
device list unchanged.

     oc-box reset --project ~/devel/demo --clear-podman-args \
       --podman-arg=--shm-size=2g

5.38 -delete-sessions
=====================

Purge-only.  Delete recorded host archive session copies as well as
environment resources.  Project files remain unless ‘--all’ is used.  It
never deletes an original host source conversation merely because that
conversation was imported into this workspace.  Example: ‘oc-box purge
--workspace WORKSPACE_ID --delete-sessions’.

5.39 -all
=========

Purge-only.  Remove environment resources, recorded host session
archives, metadata, and the entire original project directory, including
untracked files.  The directory identity and marker must still match.
Example: ‘oc-box purge --project ~/devel/disposable --all’.

5.40 -y, -yes
=============

Skip purge's ID-prefix confirmation.  Purge-only; it does not answer
OpenCode questions or bypass permissions on other commands.  Example:
‘oc-box purge --workspace WORKSPACE_ID --all --yes’.


