6 Lifecycle and persistence
***************************

     Operation              Project   Private home/sessions   Container OS
     Exit OpenCode          kept      kept; archive sync     kept; stopped
     stop                   kept      kept                   kept; stopped
     sync                   kept      kept; archive sync     kept; stopped
     terminal (V1/maintenance) kept     kept; no archive sync  kept; prior run state
     terminal (V2 attach)    kept      shared current session kept; owner controls stop
     web (V2)               kept      shared current session kept; sync/stop on last client
     reset                  kept      kept, same session ID  rebuilt
     purge                  kept      deleted                deleted
     purge --delete-sessions kept      deleted                deleted
     purge --all            deleted   deleted                deleted

Default purge retains host archive copies; ‘--delete-sessions’ and
‘--all’ remove recorded host archive copies.  Imported source host
conversations remain.  Shared base images are retained by all
operations.  Stop/reset do not checkpoint running processes.  Packages
installed in the private home survive reset; system-wide packages do
not.  No command currently manages Btrfs snapshots.

CPU, RAM, process, distro, and mapping changes require reset for
existing containers.  A stop/start alone reuses the old create-time
settings.  Avoid changing resources directly through Podman if metadata
consistency is required.  An active launcher holds a nonblocking
workspace lock; sync, reset, repair, maintenance terminals, and purge
require that launcher to exit.  V1 start also requires that launcher to
exit.  V2 TUI/terminal/web attach clients use same-user leases and can
coexist without refreshing credentials or taking the exclusive lock.
Read-only list/status and explicit stop can run from a second terminal.
A crash releases the lock when its process exits; an orphan still
running can retain it.


