8 Sessions and recovery
***********************

The live database is private container storage.  The normal host
database is not mounted.  Normal exit and ‘sync’ export root
conversations using supported mainline commands, then import namespaced
host archive IDs with titles prefixed ‘[oc-box project-name]’.  Repeated
sync refreshes the archive copy.  Resume uses the original container
session, not that archive ID.

This is conversation-history sync, not live replication or a backup
against disk loss.  Host archives omit snapshot stores, separate
subagent transcripts, running processes, installed packages, and files
outside the exported transcript.  New work in a host archive is not
merged back automatically.  Container/home storage normally survives a
client crash or reboot; the last uncommitted turn can still be lost.
Retry missed archival with ‘oc-box sync’.

OpenCode 2.x history exports are checked for unsettled messages.  A
running direct shell or compaction and an unfinished assistant message
at the end of a session are refused with a fixed reason (‘OpenCode 2.x
history is unsettled; retry sync after completion.’); that session keeps
its last good archives, every other session is still synchronized, and
‘sync’ returns nonzero with the number of pending sessions.  Retry after
the work finishes.  An unfinished assistant message that the session has
already moved past (settled messages follow it in an idle session) never
settles on its own, and native OpenCode omits it from its export.  This
is typical of history migrated from OpenCode 1.x after an interrupted or
killed turn; 2.x itself settles an older unfinished assistant when the
next turn starts.  The superseded-assistant exception also refuses a
session that is currently active.  An inactive session is archived as
that native export; the archive annotation records the omitted count and
‘sync’ prints a notice with the number of affected sessions, never their
titles, IDs or content.  Other failures show a fixed explanation when
the private history bridge can name one, otherwise only ‘podman failed
(exit N)’; native error text and private data are never printed.

For ‘--import-session’, quit the source client first.  Its project must
match the selected directory.  A private source export is saved in
‘imports/SESSION_ID.json’ under the workspace registry.  oc-box imports
into the container, verifies every message/part, then launches that
exact ID. Mainline's removal of obsolete nonpending tool ‘state.raw’
buffers is accepted, but tool inputs/outputs, pending raw buffers, text,
IDs, and metadata remain checked.

Do not routinely repeat the import flag.  An already verified ID is not
merged or overwritten.  If verification was interrupted/rejected, retry
the same import after updating the launcher: an existing session may be
recovered by comparison with the original saved export, without
reimporting over container work.  Differences outside the known
normalization stop launch and retain both copies.

     oc-box --project ~/devel/demo --import-session ses_HOST_SESSION_ID
     oc-box --project ~/devel/demo
     oc-box sync --project ~/devel/demo

The source-tree diagnostic prints counts and differing field names, not
text:

     python3 tools/diagnose_import.py --workspace WORKSPACE_ID --session SESSION_ID

8.1 Recover history after replacing a workspace marker
======================================================

Older launchers allowed removing ‘.oc-box-id’ and starting again to
create a fresh environment.  Current launchers detect the registered
path and refuse to create a duplicate; run ‘repair’ to reconnect the
existing environment instead.  Removing the marker does not delete the
old private volume, registry, or host archives.  If an older launcher
already created a replacement, to continue a previously synced
conversation in the new environment, exit its active client, find the
host archive title prefixed ‘[oc-box project-name]’, and import its ID:

     opencode --pure session list
     oc-box --project ~/devel/demo --import-session ses_ARCHIVE_ID

Mainline records host archive imports against oc-box's private
‘controller’ directory.  oc-box verifies the archive's namespaced ID
against that workspace, its original host registry, and its saved
‘history/SESSION_ID.json’ before restoring the project directory for
transfer.  Both saved records must identify the selected project.  The
current host transcript is copied, retaining any later host archive
work; the host session and saved archive are not changed.  The archive
title alone cannot authorize a different project.

After a plain purge with a current launcher, the same checks use its
private ‘retired/ID/’ recovery records if the original active registry
is gone.  Older purges that removed those records cannot be
reconstructed automatically: use a retained backup or a newer archive
from the live workspace.  Existing container sessions remain usable
without the retired registry; that registry is needed only for verifying
a host archive's old controller-directory origin.

Use the import flag once, then resume normally.  If the original
registry or saved export is missing, restore it before retrying.  Host
history contains only successfully synced turns; the old private volume
may contain newer work.  Keep the original workspace resources until
recovery is complete.  Importing history does not copy installed
packages, snapshots, or other private-home files.

8.2 Retire a stale duplicate without deleting the current project
=================================================================

Inspect the marker association and recorded sessions before cleanup:

     oc-box list
     oc-box --status
     oc-box purge --workspace STALE_WORKSPACE_ID

Keep the workspace selected by the current project marker.  Plain purge
final-syncs the stale workspace's history and removes only its owned
container/volume/network and active registry.  It leaves the replacement
workspace, project files, current marker, and host conversation archives
intact.  If the final sync or recovery-copy write fails, cleanup stops
before removing resources.  Do not use ‘--all’ or ‘--delete-sessions’
for this history-preserving duplicate cleanup.  oc-box never chooses and
deletes duplicates automatically.  If the old workspace has newer
unsynced work, recover it before retiring its private home.


