9 Devices and host permissions
******************************

The host user must be able to access the device.  Host supplementary
groups are not automatically preserved; where group access is required,
use installed crun and ‘--group-add=keep-groups’.  A same-named group
inside the container is not the host group.  Newly acquired groups
require a fresh host login/process with those groups.  Driver-specific
SDKs must be installed inside the container.

Before creating/recreating device-enabled containers, oc-box checks
requested host read/write access and SELinux's device boolean, including
‘/usr/sbin’ utilities that may be absent from PATH. Warnings are
advisory; oc-box does not run sudo or change host policy.  The policy
warning is skipped when device access is already allowed, SELinux is not
enforcing, or the container explicitly bypasses labeling.  CDI devices
receive the SELinux check, not a host-path check.

     oc-box reset --project ~/devel/npu \
       --device /dev/accel/accel0 \
       --podman-arg=--runtime=crun \
       --podman-arg=--group-add=keep-groups

SELinux can deny access even when a mapped device name is visible with
‘-?????????’ attributes.  From the host, inspect policy and permissions:

     ls -ldZ /dev/accel
     ls -lZ /dev/accel/accel0
     id
     /usr/sbin/getenforce
     /usr/sbin/getsebool container_use_devices
     test -r /dev/accel/accel0 && test -w /dev/accel/accel0

If the user has access and enforcing SELinux has the device boolean off,
Podman's documented host-wide policy setting is:

     sudo /usr/sbin/setsebool -P container_use_devices on

It permits device labels subject to mappings and Unix permissions; it
does not map extra devices or disable SELinux.  It persists and applies
immediately, including to running containers, without reset/reboot.  A
memlock problem does not explain failure to read device attributes.
Once access works, inspect the driver and limits independently inside
the container:

     ls -l /dev/accel/accel0
     prlimit --pid $$ --memlock
     xrt-smi examine

Requested memlock limits are still subject to the host user's hard limit
and kernel/runtime rules; passing a ulimit does not grant host
privileges.


