10 Resource reports
*******************

Status collects running state, CPU, RAM/limit, PIDs, uptime, configured
limits, image, storage driver, restart/exit/OOM/health information when
available, and the latest saved session and archive IDs.  Device
mappings and saved raw Podman options are included.  No container
commands run during collection.

It also checks the project's regular marker and recorded filesystem
identity, identifies multiple registered workspaces for the same project
in an all-workspace report, and includes specific repair or
history-preserving cleanup instructions.  JSON fields
‘project_marker_workspace’ and ‘related_workspace_ids’ expose those
associations.  Suspicious/missing projects are not scanned for storage
usage.  ‘Host archive: not recorded’ means this workspace has not
recorded a successful sync of its selected session yet; the live
volume/session remains intact.  Exit normally or, after the launcher
exits, run ‘sync’ to create the host archive.

Storage is split into writable OS layer, private home volume, and
project.  The rootfs size including shared image layers is separate.
Filesystem used/free space and block-I/O counters are shown.  CPU is a
short sample where 100% is one core; stopped containers have no live
sample.  Missing/unavailable values are not reported as zero.  Rootless
pasta network counters are omitted as unreliable.

Directory scans count allocated blocks without following symlinks or
crossing filesystems, with a 15-second timeout per directory.
Private-volume scans use ‘podman unshare’ on the host, not container
exec.  Very large caches may take time or report unavailable size.
Shared image layers must not be summed as unique storage.  Btrfs
compression, reflinks, snapshots, and shared extents make these
estimates different from exclusive physical usage.  There is no disk
quota.

     oc-box --status
     oc-box --status --workspace WORKSPACE_ID --json > workspace-status.json


