11 Configuration and environment
********************************

On launch, global OpenCode configuration, agents, skills, local plugins,
and MCP settings are copied into private storage; they are not host
mounts.  Project configuration runs natively inside the container.
Removed host plugins are removed from the copy.  Container dependency
caches and generated SDK locks are reused when host dependency inputs
and runtime version are unchanged.  Cold plugin resolution has its own
timed setup stage before the TUI opens.

The generated profile selects Bash and enables native formatting when
not configured, plus native LSP on 1.x only.  The V2 container-only
Build helper auto-allows final tool permission asks, preserving denies
passed to the hook.  The existing final Build wildcard still overrides
copied ordinary global/Build tool permission rules, including their
configured denies, as before.  Plan/subagent and plain host permissions
are unchanged.  V1 behavior is unchanged.  Other copied plugins may have
independent checks.  Changes to host config are copied at the next
owning launch; native V2 reload inside the container does not fetch new
host config or vault values.  Host-only executables must be installed
inside the container.  Native OpenCode saved/OAuth credential refresh
and copying remain independent in every credential mode, including
global vault mode; refreshed container auth is not written back to host
auth.  Global vault entries are environment variables, not replacements
for structured OAuth refresh-token records.  Existing copied/container
config/auth secrets remain independent; changing credential mode does
not revoke them.

The new V2 helper makes only a **final ‘ask’ to ‘allow’** conversion for
the resolved actual Build agent; it never overwrites a deny passed to
it.  An explicit permission event agent wins; when omitted, resolve the
current session's agent, then the native configured default, never the
parent's agent.  Explicit session and mandatory policy denials remain
denied, as do denials from managed rules that apply after the private
overlay.  Unknown-agent requests retain native ask/deny outcomes and are
never treated as Build.  This does not preserve ordinary copied
Build/global denies overridden by the existing Build wildcard.  All
non-Build rules remain intact.  A Build parent does not grant Build
permission treatment to Plan/child requests.  Session permission arrays
are not overwritten, so Build allowances do not leak into Plan or child
rules.  Question and MCP elicitation prompts are separate from
permission asks and may still need user input.

The final private ‘OPENCODE_CONFIG_CONTENT’ overlay reenables only the
generated Build helper against lower-priority project plugin removal
entries; managed policy still wins.  Helper activation relies on this
overlay, not on an external agent transform running after every config
source.  No ‘--auto’ or client-wide autoaccept flag is added to oc-box
clients.  The new native evaluator/import/reload fixture targets these
boundaries offline, not live host acceptance.

The container Build-only allow-all default is not native V2 client-wide
autoaccept: Plan/subagent configured rules and plain host permissions
remain unchanged.  As an optional one-off **host OpenCode** choice,
‘opencode --auto --standalone’ auto-approves permission asks, not
explicit denials, and uses normal host env/auth rather than the oc-box
vault.  In a running native TUI, Ctrl+P → Open settings → Sessions →
Permissions → autoaccept is the corresponding user setting (‘cli.json’
‘session.permissions’).  That broader preference can persist in the
client's global CLI settings; oc-box does not enable it or edit normal
host ‘cli.json’.  It is not a blanket replacement for the Build-only
default.  The separate scripted global-vault host fixture has passed
native tool/permission and reload checks.  A new interactive TUI
permission-prompt/reload pass is still pending; the scripted result is
not interactive UI acceptance or security certification.

HTTP endpoint keys in global config using localhost, 127.0.0.1, or ::1
are rewritten to ‘host.containers.internal’.  OAuth redirect URIs and
shell commands are not rewritten.  Container localhost remains local to
the container.

11.1 Environment variables
==========================

‘OC_BOX_STATE_HOME’ overrides the host workspace registry root.
Otherwise it is ‘$XDG_STATE_HOME/oc-box’, defaulting to
‘~/.local/state/oc-box’.  Use the same registry when looking up existing
workspace IDs; changing it does not migrate old resources automatically.

‘XDG_CONFIG_HOME’ selects the source global OpenCode/oc-box
configuration; default ‘~/.config’.  ‘XDG_DATA_HOME’ selects the normal
host OpenCode data root; default ‘~/.local/share’.  The installer itself
uses fixed ‘~/.local’ paths.  Container XDG paths use the private home,
not host XDG directories.

Without an explicit workspace policy, normal V2 owning launches use all
current host vault entries.  A present vault replaces ambient automatic
‘_API_KEY’/ ‘_TOKEN’ and ‘pass_env’ forwarding, including legacy
‘OPENCODE_AUTH_CONTENT’ overrides; no vault present retains env-file
fallback with setup guidance.  Ordinary native OpenCode saved/OAuth
credential refresh/copy remains independent in every mode.  V1 and
explicit ‘env’ policy retain the legacy rules below; ‘selected’ policy
instead uses only selected credential names.  ‘--no-vault’ is a
one-owning-invocation V2 compatibility opt-out, not a saved default or a
way to refresh an attached service.

Under legacy env forwarding, variables ending ‘_API_KEY’ or ‘_TOKEN’,
plus ‘GH_TOKEN’ and ‘GITHUB_TOKEN’, are passed when set.  Terminal
color/language settings and ‘LEMONADE_BASE_URL’ remain noncredential
runtime preferences.  These forwarding rules apply to normal OpenCode
launches, not maintenance terminals.  For an intentional legacy env
override, add other uppercase names (not values) in host oc-box
configuration:

     {
       "pass_env": ["MY_MCP_SETTING", "MY_PROVIDER_SECRET"]
     }

Save that as ‘~/.config/oc-box/config.json’ (or its XDG equivalent).
Only values present in the host environment are copied.  Forwarded HTTP
localhost URLs are mapped to the host-service name.  The default
‘LEMONADE_BASE_URL’ is ‘http://host.containers.internal:13305/api/v1’.
‘TERM’ defaults to ‘xterm-256color’.  Host PATH, shell startup scripts,
proxy variables, engine sockets, and SSH agents are not forwarded by
default.

Separate password creation minimums can be saved in the same JSON
object:

     {
       "vault_min_passphrase_length": 12,
       "backup_min_passphrase_length": 16
     }

Each defaults to 8 and accepts an integer from 8 to 1024.  They set a
minimum character count, not an exact size; all passphrases remain
bounded to 1024 UTF-8 bytes.  ‘--min-passphrase-length N’ overrides the
relevant setting for one creation/change.  Invalid policy fails before
vault unlock or backup lifecycle changes.  Existing passwords and
recipient-encrypted backups do not consult these creation settings.
Preserve your other keys when editing this file.

The same JSON object can include ‘"auto_update": true’ to opt in to
anonymous GitHub checks/installation before normal installed launches,
or use ‘oc-box upgrade --auto-update on’.  The setting is off by default
and is not forwarded into the container.  A successful explicit channel
selection saves the GitHub choice as ‘update_channel’ (‘stable’ or
‘devel’), or a package-owner-specific choice under
‘package_update_channels’.  Missing choices default to stable.  Use
‘upgrade --stable’/‘--devel’ rather than copying an owner key between
installations; ‘--check’ does not write these settings.  Channel
settings preserve unrelated keys and are not workspace credential
policies.

‘OC_BOX_UPDATE_CHECKED=1’ is an internal one-launch guard used when
restarting the host launcher after automatic installation; it prevents
recursion, not authentication or a workspace identity override.

Forwarded credential/custom environment values are passed through the
Podman client's environment, not embedded in its process arguments.
They are still accessible to container processes and same-user
environment inspection.  Copied configuration and auth can also contain
secrets; only supply credentials the agent should use.  See SECURITY.md
for the documented trust boundary.

11.2 Native V2 provider and MCP environment references
======================================================

Store values with ‘oc-box secrets set ANTHROPIC_API_KEY’ and ‘oc-box
secrets set MCP_API_KEY’ before the first V2 owning launch.  OpenCode
configuration contains **references only**, not secret values.  Merge
the fields you need into global ‘~/.config/opencode/opencode.json(c)’ or
project ‘opencode.json(c)’, preserving unrelated settings.  The
published V2 shapes use ‘providers.PROVIDER.settings’ and
‘mcp.servers.NAME.environment’:

     {
       "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
       "providers": {
         "anthropic": {
           "settings": {
             "apiKey": "{env:ANTHROPIC_API_KEY}"
           }
         }
       },
       "mcp": {
         "servers": {
           "example-tools": {
             "type": "local",
             "command": ["example-mcp-server"],
             "environment": {
               "MCP_API_KEY": "{env:MCP_API_KEY}"
             }
           }
         }
       }
     }

Replace the placeholder MCP executable with one installed in the
container.  Remote API-key-only MCP servers can use
‘mcp.servers.NAME.headers’ with ‘"Authorization": "Bearer
{env:MCP_API_KEY}"’ and ‘"oauth": false’; prefer native OAuth sign-in
when supported.  JSON shell expressions such as ‘$MCP_API_KEY’ are not
environment references.  Providers/MCPs resolve the owning service's
environment, and session shells/tools inherit delivered entries.  New
vault values or names require a new owner; attach and native config
reload do not replace its environment.  See the published V2 config and
MCP guides at https://opencode.ai/docs/config and
https://opencode.ai/docs/mcp-servers.

Plain ‘opencode --standalone’ is a one-off **host OpenCode** command
using normal host environment/auth, not an oc-box command and not an
oc-box vault consumer.  For one-off env-file compatibility only:

     . ~/.config/provider-credentials.env
     opencode --standalone
     # Or one owning V2 oc-box launch without vault delivery:
     oc-box --project ~/devel/demo --no-vault

The recommended primary V2 oc-box workflow is global vault-first,
without sourcing a file or registering project selections.  Native
OpenCode saved/OAuth credential refresh and copying remain independent
in every mode, including global vault mode.  oc-box never imports or
rewrites plaintext OpenCode JSON secrets into its vault.

Legacy env forwarding can use ‘OPENCODE_AUTH_CONTENT’ as a provider-auth
override; configured global vault mode suppresses this ambient override,
without disabling ordinary native OpenCode saved/OAuth credential
refresh and copying.  The launcher sets ‘OC_BOX_CONTAINER=1’,
‘OPENCODE_DISABLE_SANDBOX=1’, and ‘OPENCODE_DISABLE_AUTOUPDATE=1’ inside
it, plus its private profile/config.  OpenCode's existing Bubblewrap
plugin is disabled only for this launch; Podman is the environment
boundary.  No OpenCode fork is installed.


