12 Files
********

12.1 Host installation
======================

‘~/.local/bin/oc-box’ is the launcher.  ‘~/.local/share/oc-box/’
contains installed Python sources, documentation, LICENSE, NOTICE, and
embedded ‘build-info.json’.  New installations stage complete versioned
copies under ‘releases/’ and use a ‘current’ symlink plus compatibility
pointers for the documented top-level files.  The launcher pins a
release path so other active launchers keep their code unchanged.  Old
copies are retained, not automatically deleted.  When first switching
from an older flat installation, exit old active launchers before
reinstalling; only launchers installed with release-path pinning have
this concurrent-update guarantee.  The source checkout and installation
are independent; use ‘upgrade’ or reinstall after updates.
‘~/.local/share/man/man1/oc-box.1’ and ‘~/.local/share/info/oc-box.info’
are the installed manuals.  Installation requires no man/Info build
tools.

12.2 Host registry and archives
===============================

‘~/.local/state/oc-box/workspaces/ID/workspace.json’ holds directory
identity, container/volume names, distro, limits, and saved
Podman/device settings.  Explicit credential policy contains mode and
selected names only, never values.  Global vault mode does not save an
automatic entry-name list.  ‘project_identity’ retains the last verified
device/inode for diagnostics; ‘project_filesystem_identity’, when
supported, records ‘linux-file-handle-v1’ filesystem ID and opaque
persistent directory handle.  Driver-provided handles can include
generation, dataset/subvolume, or other identifying information; oc-box
neither decodes them nor selects a format by filesystem name.  The
transient mount ID is not recorded.  The registry need not share the
project's filesystem: no hard links or filesystem-specific utilities are
used.  ‘sessions.json’ (1.x) and ‘sessions-v2.json’ (2.x) hold separate
session IDs and archive associations.  ‘imports/’ and ‘imports-v2/’ hold
private source exports; ‘history/’ holds private host archive JSON
files.  V2 resume state also records missing source IDs without removing
their saved archive associations.  Archive relationship annotations
remain in ‘history/’ and host transcript metadata, including private
provenance retained by plain purge.  ‘lock’ serializes mutating
commands.  These files are outside the project and not mounted into the
container.  Transcript files may contain private content.

‘.oc-box-id’ in the project associates the directory with its registry.
Do not edit or copy it to authorize a different mount.  Identity checks
include the canonical path, inode, and supported filesystem
ID/persistent handle; older or unsupported filesystems use the
conservative device/inode pair.  A verified device-only change does not
by itself invalidate a stable directory baseline.  Deleting/recreating
the same path or losing its marker does not silently select the old
environment or create a duplicate while an active registration remains.
An explicitly requested ‘repair’ can accept the current identity at the
same path and restore a missing marker, retaining the existing
environment.  With several registrations, select the intended workspace
by ID; never copy markers between unrelated directories.

Plain purge retains verified archive recovery evidence under
‘~/.local/state/oc-box/retired/ID/’, outside the active workspace
registry.  It contains only minimal ID/project provenance and private
‘history/’ JSON files, not container storage, auth, installed
dependencies, or the full live database.  Keep it while those host
archives may need importing; it is private saved data, not a project
file or active environment.  It has no automatic expiry.

The normal host OpenCode database, usually under
‘~/.local/share/opencode/’, contains archive copies.  Old installations
may retain one-time relocation backups under
‘~/.local/state/oc-box/migrations/’; these are private data, not
workspace resources or release artifacts, and later workspace purge
leaves them intact.  The original development-only relocation helper is
retired.

12.3 Container data
===================

The home volume is ‘oc-box-home-ID’, mounted at the host user's home
pathname.  OpenCode data lives under its ‘.local/share/opencode/’.
Copied configuration and generated ‘oc-box-profile.json’ live under
‘.config/opencode/’.  The matching OpenCode executable is
‘/opt/oc-box/runtime/bin/opencode’; Node/npm, Python/uv, Bash, Git,
ripgrep, curl, archive tools, and PDF tools are provided by baseline
setup.  Additional system tools belong to the container OS layer.


