14 Examples
***********

14.1 Everyday start and resume
==============================

     mkdir -p ~/devel/demo
     oc-box --project ~/devel/demo
     oc-box --project ~/devel/demo
     oc-box --project ~/devel/demo --new

The second launch resumes work.  A new conversation does not reset files
or installed tools.  Exit OpenCode normally to synchronize history and
stop it.

14.2 Inspect from a second terminal
===================================

     oc-box list
     oc-box --status
     oc-box --status --project ~/devel/demo --json > status.json
     podman ps -a --filter label=io.oc-box.managed=true

14.3 Grow an existing environment without losing progress
=========================================================

Exit its client before reset.  Preserve source, private home, and
session IDs, but reinstall project-specific system packages afterward:

     oc-box reset --project ~/devel/demo --cpus 8 --memory 16g --pids-limit 4096

14.4 Continue a host session and recover after a crash
======================================================

     oc-box --project ~/devel/demo --import-session ses_HOST_SESSION_ID
     oc-box --project ~/devel/demo
     oc-box sync --project ~/devel/demo

Import once; normal launches use the saved container session afterward.

14.5 Replace persistent hardware settings
=========================================

     oc-box reset --project ~/devel/npu --clear-devices --device /dev/accel/accel0 \
       --clear-podman-args --podman-arg=--runtime=crun \
       --podman-arg=--group-add=keep-groups

14.6 Remove a disposable project completely
===========================================

     oc-box purge --project ~/devel/disposable --all
     oc-box list

If the directory was deleted manually, use its workspace ID instead:

     oc-box list
     oc-box purge --workspace WORKSPACE_ID --all

The last command deletes its remaining managed resources and recorded
host archives; an unrelated replacement directory will not be deleted.


