15 Troubleshooting
******************

15.1 Private output capture and uv Python
=========================================

V2 startup copies native OpenCode saved/OAuth credentials independently
of the oc-box vault.  ‘--no-vault’ skips vault entries, not native
authentication or the private output capture that protects it.  There is
currently no oc-box ‘--verbose’ flag; the final error after cleanup
explains a failed startup stage.

Some older uv-managed Python builds omit ‘os.memfd_create’ even on
capable Linux hosts.  oc-box first uses the Python binding, or, when it
is absent, lazily calls libc's ‘memfd_create’ with close-on-exec
enabled.  Both paths use the same kernel facility, never disk-backed
temporary files or architecture-specific raw syscalls.  A denied/failed
native call is not retried through libc.  Capability checks happen
before container startup or reset deletion; preflight failures leave the
existing container and workspace intact.

     oc-box doctor --feature private-output

If the capability is still unavailable, the error identifies the Python
running oc-box.  For a ‘uv tool install’ installation, compare it with
system Python:

     "$(uv tool dir)/oc-box/bin/python" -c 'import os,sys; print(sys.executable); print(sys.version); print("memfd_create:", hasattr(os, "memfd_create"))'
     /usr/bin/python3 -c 'import os,sys; print(sys.version); print("memfd_create:", hasattr(os, "memfd_create"))'

An absent Python attribute alone is no longer a failure if the libc path
works.  If system Python is 3.10+ and supports private capture, recreate
only uv's tool environment with it, retaining any extras originally
installed:

     uv tool install --force --python /usr/bin/python3 oc-box

This does not install into system Python's site-packages.  Retry the
original launch; no reset, purge, vault setup, or workspace-image change
is needed.  A kernel or security-policy denial still fails closed;
switching interpreters cannot bypass such restrictions.  Backup/restore
uses the same capability helper.

For a disposable host validation of V2 global-vault first-launch
delivery, Build approvals/reload and full backup/restore, use the
source-checkout fixture:

     uv run --isolated --no-project --with cryptography python -I -B tools/test_global_vault_podman.py \
       --opencode-v2 "$(command -v opencode)" \
       --distro tumbleweed --work-dir /var/tmp --temp-dir /var/tmp

Run from the repository root in a regular host terminal, outside oc-box
and other shell sandboxes, without sudo.  This creates fresh private
synthetic vault/history/projects and verified separate Podman
graphroot/runroot state; it does not install a launcher or use normal
host credentials/services.  Choose existing disk-backed work/temp
parents with enough space/quota.  TUI/prompt input is scripted; this is
not interactive TUI or comprehensive security proof.  The fixture
retains its report, resources and encrypted evidence.  Use only its
printed label/store-validated cleanup command, never global prune or
manual marker deletion.  See ‘docs/opencode-2.md’ for exact scope.  No
new live result is accepted merely by preparing the fixture or passing
its offline unit tests.

15.2 Reboot, remount, and directory identity changes
====================================================

Clearing ‘~/.cache’ does not erase the oc-box registry (normally under
‘~/.local/state/oc-box’) or private Podman volumes, and it does not
change a directory's identity.  Linux device numbers can change across
reboot/remount; Btrfs commonly assigns a different ‘st_dev’ while the
directory inode stays the same.  Those mount-local numbers are not
persistent disk identifiers.

The algorithm is capability-based through Linux's common VFS interfaces,
not Btrfs-specific.  It uses no ext4, XFS, Btrfs, bcachefs, or
ZFS-specific parser, ioctl, tool, or device path.  On filesystems
exposing ‘name_to_handle_at’, oc-box combines the directory's opaque
persistent handle with the ‘statvfs’ filesystem ID and inode.  Drivers
supply their own handle encoding; different formats and bounded variable
sizes are accepted without a filesystem allowlist.  If this stored
identity matches and only the device number changed, launch/list/status
accept the same directory automatically.  No cache deletion, repair,
reset, or session import is needed.  Normal lifecycle launch updates the
last verified device/inode while holding the workspace lock; read-only
listings do not write metadata.  No extra command-line tools, mount
operations, or elevated capability are required for the handle lookup.
The private registry and project may be on different filesystems, and a
project's bind-mount location need not be the filesystem root.

New workspaces record that baseline at creation.  Matching old
registrations are upgraded during the next normal
start/sync/reset/terminal, or by explicit repair.  If an old registry
already differs after reboot and lacks the stored baseline, run one
explicit repair after checking the directory; the launcher cannot safely
infer the original filesystem from an old device number and matching
inode.  Keeping the same inode on a different filesystem is not enough.

If persistent handles are unsupported (including some overlay/network
mounts), or the filesystem ID is unavailable/zero, the existing strict
device/inode check remains.  Driver/kernel support, not the filesystem's
name, determines this behavior.  oc-box does not claim zero-repair
reboot compatibility with every possible filesystem driver.  Once a
strong baseline is recorded, an unavailable or different handle does not
downgrade to a weaker check: it requires explicit repair.
Filesystem/subvolume/snapshot restoration, inode/generation changes,
copied markers, and mount replacements remain recovery decisions for the
host operator.  Older diagnostics incorrectly treated any changed device
number as a replaced directory; do not remove the marker.

A filesystem-independent logical ID would be just the canonical path
plus a marker checked against the host registry.  That works for
selecting a workspace, but it cannot distinguish an exact copy/restore
of that path and marker from the original directory.  A signed marker
has the same limitation because copies carry the signature too.  oc-box
deliberately keeps physical replacement checks and explicit repair when
the OS cannot provide enough evidence, rather than silently weakening
the trust boundary on an unsupported driver.  Clone/migration/restore
operations are explicit repair cases, not ordinary reboot cases.

Keep the marker to retain the association with the existing environment.
Update/reinstall the launcher, check that the project at the registered
path is correct, and exit its active client.  Then run from a host
terminal:

     oc-box repair --project ~/devel/demo
     oc-box --project ~/devel/demo --resume

If the container is still running, use ‘oc-box stop --workspace
WORKSPACE_ID’ and retry repair.  Repair preserves the container, private
volume, and session IDs.  It does not recover storage that was deleted
separately.

If the marker was already deleted, current launchers search the registry
by canonical project path and print existing IDs/session information
instead of creating a fresh environment.  To restore a selected
association:

     oc-box --status
     oc-box repair --workspace WORKSPACE_ID
     oc-box --workspace WORKSPACE_ID --resume

If multiple records share the path, a path-only repair does not guess.
Choose the intended workspace by ID; another workspace's existing marker
is not overwritten.  If a replacement is already in use, retain it and
remove the stale record with plain ID-selected purge as described in
Sessions and recovery.

If the marker has no matching host registry, check ‘OC_BOX_STATE_HOME’
and ‘XDG_STATE_HOME’ and restore the original registry before resuming.
The default registry is ‘~/.local/state/oc-box’, separate from
‘~/.cache’.

15.3 Missing delegated CPU or memory controllers
================================================

On some openSUSE hosts a vendor user-manager drop-in clears delegation.
Startup then fails on ‘memory.swap.max’ or missing cgroup controllers
despite kernel support.  The launcher reports this before starting the
container.  From a host terminal, substitute your numeric UID for 1000:

     sudo systemctl edit user@1000.service --drop-in=90-oc-box-delegate.conf

Save the override:

     [Service]
     Delegate=cpu cpuset io memory pids

Run ‘sudo systemctl daemon-reload’.  If the current user manager still
lacks the controllers, fully end/restart it through a fresh login or
reboot.  A reboot is not a blanket Podman installation requirement.
Check effective delegation in
‘/sys/fs/cgroup/user.slice/user-1000.slice/user@1000.service/cgroup.controllers’.
Retry the existing workspace without reset or purge.  Do not edit vendor
files.

15.4 Project mount denied by SELinux
====================================

New mounts use Podman's shared ‘:z’ project relabeling.  Older mounts
can exist but deny file access.  Relabel only the project from the host:

     chcon -R -t container_file_t /path/to/project

Resume normally; no session reimport/reset is needed.  Device-node
labeling is separate: use the device-policy diagnostics in Devices and
host permissions.

15.5 Slow first launch or blank terminal
========================================

Cold creation performs package and plugin downloads.  Timed stage output
and the temporary TUI loading line indicate progress.  Warm starts
retain compatible dependency state.  Host SDK manifests older than
runtime are normalized inside the copied config, not by modifying host
configuration.  Preserve the environment when diagnosing a wait; reset
discards the OS cache and can repeat cold setup.

15.6 Active workspace lock or failed import
===========================================

Exit the active launcher before sync/reset/repair/maintenance/purge.
Ordinary V2 TUI, session shell terminals and web clients can join the
live owner.  Status and stop remain available in another terminal.  Do
not delete the lock file to bypass a live process.  For a failed import,
retry the same flag with an updated launcher; recovery uses the saved
export without overwriting existing container work.  For a failed
archive sync, use ‘sync’; the original live DB remains in its volume.  A
deleted private volume cannot be recovered from session metadata alone.

15.7 Maintenance shell environment
==================================

Use ‘oc-box terminal --maintenance’ to debug an existing container
without launching OpenCode or supplying host tokens.  A missing command
may need installing in that container; terminal does not install
baseline tools automatically.  Its shell deliberately skips user
profiles, shell history, and normal credential forwarding.  A missing
project marker or changed identity still requires explicit ‘repair’;
terminal cannot bypass those checks.  If it reports an active launcher,
exit OpenCode and wait for its history sync/stop before retrying.  Do
not remove the workspace lock file to bypass the running process.


