16 Installation and manuals
***************************

Requires Linux, Python 3.10 or later, rootless Podman with pasta
networking, delegated cgroup CPU/memory/PIDs controllers, and host
OpenCode 1.x or 2.x.  Session import/export compatibility was checked
with OpenCode 1.19.5 and 2.2.6.  Other major OpenCode versions are
rejected rather than assuming database/CLI compatibility.  Podman must
work from the regular host user, not through sudo.  Network access is
needed for initial distribution, runtime, and dependency downloads.

16.1 Bash completion
====================

Enable command/option completion in the current Bash session, for any
installation channel (including uv and pipx):

     source <(oc-box completion bash)

Add that line to ‘~/.bashrc’ after the PATH setup for oc-box to enable
it in new interactive Bash shells.  The installer never edits your shell
startup files.  Alternatively, with the optional bash-completion
framework enabled, save the script in its per-user autoload directory:

     mkdir -p "${XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share}/bash-completion/completions"
     oc-box completion bash > "${XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share}/bash-completion/completions/oc-box"

The framework's OS package is normally ‘bash-completion’ on all seven
supported distros.  It must itself be enabled by your distro's Bash
startup setup for autoloading; directly sourcing the script works
without that framework.  Reload with the ‘source’ command after an
upgrade in an already-open shell.  If you saved a copy manually from a
uv/pipx install, regenerate that copy after upgrading.

GitHub/source installs place it in
‘~/.local/share/bash-completion/completions/oc-box’ and refresh it on
upgrades.  They also retain a release-local copy at
‘completions/oc-box’.  Wheels contain the same package resource and
install another copy under their Python environment's
‘share/bash-completion/completions/oc-box’; uv/pipx private environments
may not be on Bash's autoload search path, so use either activation
method above.  From a source checkout, ‘source completions/oc-box’ works
directly in Bash.

Completion suggests commands, context-appropriate options, vault
actions, distros/aliases, doctor features, upgrade channel selectors,
auto-update and credential-mode choices.  Credential modes include
‘env’, ‘selected’, and ‘vault’; ‘--no-vault’ is offered for
start/reset/web only (including the default start context).  Completion
does not inspect the OpenCode major version or whether a service is
already active; the command itself rejects V1 or attached-client use of
‘--no-vault’.  ‘--project’ completes directories; backup archive inputs,
‘--output’, and ‘--identity’ complete paths, including spaces.  Both
‘--option VALUE’ and ‘--option=VALUE’ are supported.  Press Tab after
‘oc-box ’ or ‘oc-box backup --’ to see candidates.  Completion uses only
Bash builtins and local pathname expansion: it does not run oc-box on
each Tab, query Podman, contact providers, read vault contents, or guess
passwords, credential names, session/workspace IDs, or model
identifiers.

16.2 Python package installations
=================================

Selected releases are available at https://pypi.org/project/oc-box/.
Choose one package-managed CLI installation, not system pip:

     pipx install oc-box
     pipx ensurepath
     # Or:
     uv tool install oc-box
     uv tool update-shell

Open a new terminal if those commands add the tool-manager bin directory
to PATH. For standard pip, create and activate a dedicated virtual
environment:

     python3 -m venv ~/.venvs/oc-box
     . ~/.venvs/oc-box/bin/activate
     python -m pip install oc-box
     oc-box --version

On later visits, activate it again or call ‘~/.venvs/oc-box/bin/oc-box’
directly.

Podman, OpenCode, Linux/cgroup configuration, and device drivers are
external host requirements, not Python dependencies.  Package
installation never creates the curl installer's ‘~/.local/share/oc-box’
release tree or a competing global wrapper.  pipx/uv expose the
package's console entrypoint in their normal bin directory.  ‘python -m
oc_box’ is also available inside the package environment.

‘oc-box upgrade’ delegates to the verified owner of the installed
environment; channel selectors and ‘--check’ also work for packages.
Direct manager commands remain available:

     oc-box upgrade --check
     oc-box upgrade --devel
     oc-box upgrade --stable
     pipx upgrade oc-box
     uv tool upgrade oc-box
     # Inside the installed virtual environment:
     python -m pip install --upgrade oc-box

The regular GitHub/curl installer and updater remain supported.  Both
methods read the same workspace registry, projects, and private
containers.  If several launchers exist, use ‘command -v oc-box’ to
check which PATH entry is selected; avoid keeping conflicting launchers
in the same bin location.  Source commits continue to publish on GitHub
independently of selected PyPI releases.

Wheels include runtime scripts, prebuilt manuals, license/notice, and
source metadata in the isolated ‘oc_box’ namespace.  Manuals also
install under the Python environment's ‘share/man/man1’ and ‘share/info’
directories; tool-manager environments are not necessarily in the host
readers' default search path.  For example, after activating the
relevant environment:

     man "$VIRTUAL_ENV/share/man/man1/oc-box.1"
     info --file "$VIRTUAL_ENV/share/info/oc-box.info"

No source checkout, setuptools, documentation build tools, or
third-party Python runtime dependency is required to use an installed
wheel.  For building or publishing, see CONTRIBUTING.md: GitHub's
‘publish.yml’ workflow uses the ‘pypi’ environment and PyPI Trusted
Publishing without long-lived upload tokens.

16.3 GitHub/curl installations
==============================

One-line installation of a published stable GitHub release requires
curl, Git, and Python 3.10+ already available on the host.  It installs
oc-box, not Podman or host OpenCode, and executes the downloaded
installer as your user:

     curl -fsSL --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 https://raw.githubusercontent.com/groopcd/oc-box/master/install.sh | sh

The bootstrap uses the saved GitHub channel, defaulting to stable on a
first installation.  Pass ‘sh -s -- --stable’ explicitly to select
stable, or use ‘sh -s -- --devel’ to select a published development
prerelease.  It supports ‘--with-vault’ alongside either channel
selector:

     curl -fsSL --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 https://raw.githubusercontent.com/groopcd/oc-box/master/install.sh | sh -s -- --devel --with-vault

‘--stable’ and ‘--devel’ cannot be combined.  Current stable 1.6 does
not have a ‘--with-tui’ option; development support is separate.
Channel-aware bootstrap selects published releases, not whichever commit
happens to be branch HEAD.

To inspect before execution instead of piping to a shell:

     curl -fsSL --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 https://raw.githubusercontent.com/groopcd/oc-box/master/install.sh -o install-oc-box.sh
     # Review install-oc-box.sh, then:
     sh install-oc-box.sh

The bootstrap clones full history anonymously into a private temporary
directory and removes that temporary checkout when finished.  It does
not configure Git authentication, download credentials, or keep a source
checkout in your project.  When moving from an older flat installation,
exit its active oc-box launchers first; future versioned installations
can coexist with already running versions.

Alternatively install from the source checkout as your host user:

     python3 install.py
     oc-box --version
     man oc-box
     info oc-box

The installer copies prebuilt manual files and does not require
makeinfo, groff, man, or info.  Reading them needs the host reader; on
openSUSE those are the ‘man’ and ‘info’ packages.  It updates only the
user's Info index when an ‘install-info’ utility is available, not any
system index.  Ensure ‘~/.local/bin’ is on PATH. If local documentation
directories are not searched:

     MANPATH="$HOME/.local/share/man:" man oc-box
     INFOPATH="$HOME/.local/share/info:" info oc-box
     man "$HOME/.local/share/man/man1/oc-box.1"
     info --file "$HOME/.local/share/info/oc-box.info"

The full CLI reference is ‘docs/cli.md’; the source tree includes
generated ‘docs/oc-box.1’, ‘docs/oc-box.texi’, and ‘docs/oc-box.info’.
Maintainers regenerate them with ‘python3 tools/build_docs.py’; that
build requires GNU makeinfo.  ‘python3 tools/build_docs.py --check’
checks the generated man/Texinfo source.  End users do not need to
rebuild documentation to install it.


